WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.480
As you may have noticed, I'm
a little bit less innovative

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.130
in my lecturing them,
Percy, so you're

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.290
going to get PowerPoint
slides rather

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:14.570
than executable
Python ones, but you

00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:18.570
should be able to find the
PDFs on the website as well.

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.200
So I've titled this lecture,
"Everything You Didn't Want

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.340
To Know About LM
Architecture And Training,"

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.007
because we're going to get into
some of the nitty-gritty details

00:00:26.007 --> 00:00:29.360
that I think most other classes
would spare you the details of.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.740
Like what should my
hyperparameters be?

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:33.920
And those kinds of questions.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.990
Some minor logistics.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:37.590
Also, if you're doing
the assignments,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.850
we are updating assignments
as we find mostly minor bugs.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:47.510
Make sure you pull updates to
the assignments as you go along.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.730
So what we're going
to do, we're going

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.560
to start with a quick
recap of a transformer.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.380
And I'll give you two variants
of a standard transformer,

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:59.890
one that's probably coming
from the standard transformer

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:02.440
lectures that you
might see in 224N.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:05.050
And then I'll talk
about what you implement

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:08.210
and the modern consensus
variant of a transformer.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.750
And then we're going to
take a much more data-driven

00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.715
perspective to understanding
transformer architectures.

00:01:13.715 --> 00:01:15.340
So the question that
we're going to ask

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:18.890
is people have trained
lots of LLMs at this point,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.340
and you can go and read
all of those papers

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.210
and try to understand
what has changed,

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:24.470
what has been in common.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.400
And from that, almost an
evolutionary analysis,

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.800
try to understand what are
the things that are really

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.460
important to make
transformers work.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.270
So today's theme is--
the theme of the class

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.270
is the best way to learn
is hands-on experience.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.978
But the theme of this
lecture, because we

00:01:37.978 --> 00:01:39.490
can't train all
these transformers,

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:43.300
is to learn from the
experience of others.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.900
So the starting point is
the original transformer.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.070
So just as a review, hopefully,
you all remember this from 224N

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.850
or your other NLP classes.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.450
You've got some simple position
embeddings at the bottom.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.390
You've got multi-head attention.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.618
You've got LayerNorms
afterwards.

00:02:00.618 --> 00:02:02.410
You've got a residual
stream going upwards.

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.650
You've got an MLP, and then
a softmax at the very end.

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:08.550
And we're going to see variants
to all of these different pieces

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:11.850
until we get to basically
the most modern variants

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.000
of the transformer.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.610
And the latest one
I'll talk about

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.820
will be just a
few months before.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.950
So what you implemented is not
the vanilla transformer variant

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:24.630
from the original paper.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:26.730
We've modified a few things.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.750
We've put the LayerNorm
in front of the block.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.050
So you can see on
this slide over here

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.910
that there's the
norm is over here

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.570
right before each of these
blocks in the residual stream.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:39.900
We've asked you to implement
rotary position embeddings.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.080
The feed-forward layers use
something called a SwiGLU.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:48.000
And then linear layers
now emit these bias terms.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:50.100
And you might ask,
why have you forced

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.860
us to implement this weird
variant of a transformer instead

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.140
of the original transformer
is all you need transformer?

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.625
And so we're going to go
through some of those questions.

00:02:59.625 --> 00:03:01.250
And then yesterday,
I was thinking, OK,

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.140
I should catch up on
all the developments

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.670
that have happened
in architectures

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:05.842
over the last year.

00:03:05.842 --> 00:03:07.800
And Percy warned me about
this because he said,

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:09.810
you're going to have to
redo the lecture every year.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:10.800
And so I started looking.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.950
And I was like, all right, yeah,
there's a couple of good papers

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:13.450
recently.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.990
There's Command A,
there's 2 OLMo 2 Furious.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.783
There's SmolLM and Phi-4.

00:03:17.783 --> 00:03:19.950
And then you go looking,
and you're like, wow, yeah,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.910
there's Gemma 3 and
Qwen 2.5 and InternLM.

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:24.510
And then we got more.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.740
I can't even cover the
screen with these guys.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.490
There's a lot of models.

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.240
There were about 19 new
dense model releases

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.205
in the last year, many of them
with minor architecture tweaks.

00:03:36.205 --> 00:03:37.580
And on the one
hand, it's kind of

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.260
annoying to go through
all these papers

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.920
and say, like, what is
happening in all of these?

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.230
But also, it's like actually
a wealth of information

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:47.010
because not all of
them do the same thing.

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:48.225
And you can see--

00:03:48.225 --> 00:03:49.850
not all of you,
especially in the back,

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:52.160
can see the details
of this slide.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.430
But I put together
a little spreadsheet

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.330
of what all these
models are doing.

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.430
And starting with all
the way from 2017,

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:01.280
the original transformer,
all the way to 2025,

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:02.980
what the newest
models are doing.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:05.060
And we'll talk
about this as we go.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.140
But you see certain kinds
of architecture changes

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.350
being explored.

00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:11.438
So here, on this column,
is position embeddings.

00:04:11.438 --> 00:04:12.980
People used to do
all sorts of stuff,

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.460
like absolute,
relative, RoPE There was

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:18.410
a alibi phase for some people.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:21.640
But then, now, starting around
2023, everyone just does RoPE.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:23.950
So you can see this
convergent evolution

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.970
almost of neural architectures.

00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:27.460
And we're going
to talk about all

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:28.918
of these different
kinds of things.

00:04:28.918 --> 00:04:33.190
So the parts that
I'll cover-- so this

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:35.913
is a preview of the three
major sections of this lecture.

00:04:35.913 --> 00:04:37.330
And if I have time,
I'm also going

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:40.520
to talk about different
attention variants at the end.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:43.190
The first thing is going to
be architecture variations.

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:44.690
That's what I'm
going to talk about.

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.910
So activations, feed-forwards,
attention variants,

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:49.060
position embeddings,
all of those things.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.920
And then having nailed down
the architecture, what do we

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:52.420
have to do?

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:54.420
Well, we have to
pick hyperparameters.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.460
How big do we make
the hidden dimension?

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:01.375
How big do we make the inner
projection layer inside of MLP?

00:05:01.375 --> 00:05:03.250
What do we do about the
number of dimensions?

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:04.360
How many vocab elements?

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:06.030
Those are all important
things that you

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.610
have to choose when you're
actually training your language

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:10.290
model.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:12.450
And you don't want to just
pick these out of a hat.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.040
You want to select them in
some fairly intelligent way.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.250
So we're going to start with
architecture variations.

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:24.130
And the two things that
I'll mention right here--

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.320
and I'll go back
to them as I talk--

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.520
the first one is, there's
not that much consensus

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:30.910
in a lot of the choices.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.630
There's been convergent
evolution in the last few years,

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:37.050
what I'll call LLaMA-like
architectures at the very bottom

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:37.593
here.

00:05:37.593 --> 00:05:39.010
But people do all
sorts of things.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:40.930
They swap between
LayerNorm and RMS Norm.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:42.790
They do serial versus
parallel layers.

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.970
There's one choice
that basically everyone

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.880
does since the very first GPT.

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.300
And I'll talk about
that in a bit.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:52.280
But there's lots of
different variations

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:54.770
that we can learn from here.

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:58.070
The big one, I've already
talked about this guy in 224N.

00:05:58.070 --> 00:06:01.040
So if you remember that lecture,
this will be review for you

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:02.700
rather than being totally new.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:06.350
I think the one thing basically
everyone agrees on and agreed

00:06:06.350 --> 00:06:09.230
on almost from the
very start, is the use

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:12.170
of pre-norm versus post-norm.

00:06:12.170 --> 00:06:15.050
That terminology will get a
little bit more confusing.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:17.000
But the original
transformer paper

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.460
did this thing on
the left over here

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:22.280
where you had your residual
stream in the gray.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:24.890
And in addition to
the residual stream,

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.740
you had these LayerNorms
after every subcomponent.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.490
So you would do your
multi-head attention.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.320
You would add back to
the residual stream.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.550
And then you would
LayerNorm that.

00:06:32.550 --> 00:06:35.180
And then you would do the same
thing with your fully connected

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:35.550
layer.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.967
And then you would
LayerNorm that.

00:06:36.967 --> 00:06:40.850
And very, very early
on, people realized

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:43.280
that moving this
LayerNorm to the front

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.160
of this non-residual part,
so this block on the right

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.510
did much better in
many different ways.

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:52.710
And basically, almost
all modern LMs that I

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.590
use this kind of pre-norm.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:57.690
There have been
some new innovations

00:06:57.690 --> 00:07:00.480
recently that I'll
touch on in two slides.

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.580
But lots of models
have moved to this.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:07.710
The one exception is
OPT350M, which I'm guessing,

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.160
they messed that one up.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.800
And that was orphaned
when they were training.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:15.960
That was a fun find in my
survey of architectures.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:18.100
So this pre versus
post-norm thing,

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.790
if you look into why it
was originally developed,

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:24.840
the arguments were that if you
wanted to use this post norm

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.770
stuff, it was much less stable.

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:29.550
And so you would have to do some
careful learning rate warm up

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:32.760
style things to make it
train in a stable way.

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:35.820
And so if you look at some
of the earlier papers arguing

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.990
for this pre-norm approach,
Salazar and Ngyuen and also

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.780
this Xiong 2020 paper,
you almost always

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.400
see this comparison of,
hey, if we use pre-norm

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:48.750
and we do some other
stability-inducing tricks,

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.230
then we can remove
warmup and these systems

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:53.390
work just as well,
if not better,

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:57.410
than the post-normal
LayerNorm with

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:58.980
careful warmup-type approaches.

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:02.150
And you see this in a machine
translation setting here.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:07.110
You see this as well on the
right on various other tasks,

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:11.420
especially using BERT which
was trained with post-norm.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:15.462
So there were many arguments
about why this was helpful.

00:08:15.462 --> 00:08:17.420
There were arguments
about gradient attenuation

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:18.003
across layers.

00:08:18.003 --> 00:08:21.110
Like if you do pre-norm,
then the gradient sizes

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:22.080
will remain constant.

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:25.460
Whereas if you did
post-norm without warm-up,

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:27.900
then it would blow up
in this orange way.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.250
It's a reasonable argument.

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:32.299
But I think a maybe more
closer to modern intuition

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:35.299
would be this argument that
pre-norm is just a more

00:08:35.299 --> 00:08:37.080
stable architecture to train.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:40.190
And so some of the earlier
work by Salazar and Ngyuen

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:43.720
identified all these loss
spikes that if you were training

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.500
with pre-norm, in blue here, you
would see a lot more loss spikes

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:51.067
and the training would be
kind of unstable as you

00:08:51.067 --> 00:08:51.650
were training.

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:53.980
So you see the
gradient norm here

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:57.640
is spiking and generally higher
than the one with pre-norm.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:01.750
And so, today, you see pre-norm
and other LayerNorm tricks

00:09:01.750 --> 00:09:07.510
being used essentially as
a stability-inducing aids

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:11.230
for using training
large neural networks.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:16.468
And so this brings us to one
new fairly recent innovation.

00:09:16.468 --> 00:09:18.010
I think this didn't
exist when I gave

00:09:18.010 --> 00:09:22.450
this lecture last year, which is
this variant that I don't think

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:24.220
really has a great
name, but I'm just

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:26.750
going to call it the double
norm for the moment here.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:29.260
So this is the original
figure that I showed you

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:30.200
at the very beginning.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:31.658
And we know that
putting LayerNorms

00:09:31.658 --> 00:09:33.700
in the residual stream is bad.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:37.400
But actually, someone in two
224N this year asked, well,

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.770
but why do you have to put
the LayerNorm in the front?

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:42.460
Why can't you put it after
the feed-forward network?

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:43.750
And of course, you can.

00:09:43.750 --> 00:09:48.000
And not only that, recent
people have gone around and just

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.890
add the LayerNorm after
the blocks as well.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:55.410
And so Grok and Gemma 2 both
take this approach of LayerNorms

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.840
both in front and after.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.660
Olmo 2 two does
only the LayerNorm

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.520
after the feed-forward in
the multi-head attention.

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.910
And so this is actually
an interesting change.

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:09.955
Pre-norm has just been dominant
and the only thing for a while.

00:10:09.955 --> 00:10:11.830
But things have been
changed up a little bit.

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:13.840
So now, there's a new variant.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.350
And there's been
some evaluations

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:17.650
of this kind of approach.

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:20.490
People have argued it's a
little bit more stable and nicer

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:23.400
to train on these larger models.

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:26.920
By the way, feel free to stop
me and ask me questions as well.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:30.160
I have a tendency to keep
going if no one stops me.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:31.080
So, yes?

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.930
Why is LayerNorm in
the residual bad?

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.770
Why is LayerNorm in
the residual bad?

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:37.480
That's a good question.

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:39.710
I don't think I
can give you a this

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:41.340
is the proof of why it's bad.

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:44.420
I think one intuitive argument
for why this might be bad

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:47.390
is that the residual gives you
this identity connection all

00:10:47.390 --> 00:10:49.200
the way from almost
the top of the network,

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:50.550
all the way to the bottom.

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:52.800
And so if you're trying to
train really deep networks,

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.070
this makes gradient
propagation very easy.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:57.320
So there's lots of
arguments about how

00:10:57.320 --> 00:11:00.260
LSTMs and these other
kinds of state space models

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:02.370
have difficulty propagating
gradients backwards.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:04.770
An identity connection does
not have any such problems.

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:06.950
And so putting
LayerNorms in the middle

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:09.270
might mess with that kind
of gradient behavior.

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:11.460
And that of course,
you see back here.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:13.340
This is exactly the
kind of plot you expect

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.890
to see if that's happening.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:19.550
Cool.

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:23.030
The other thing
that people now do

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:27.030
is in the original transformer,
people did LayerNorm.

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.970
And so LayerNorm is
this equation over here.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:33.300
What you do is you have the
activations x coming in.

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:35.490
You subtract the empirical mean.

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:37.520
So that's the average
of the x's up top.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.870
And then you divide by the
standard or the variance

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:42.590
plus a little fudge
factor epsilon.

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:43.840
And then you square root that.

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:46.520
So that you can roughly think
of it as a standard deviation.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:50.285
So that's going to standardize
your activations x.

00:11:50.285 --> 00:11:51.910
You're going to scale
it up by a gamma.

00:11:51.910 --> 00:11:53.210
That's a learnable parameter.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:54.680
And then shift it by a beta.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:56.060
So this makes sense.

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.852
You're going to normalize
your activations.

00:11:57.852 --> 00:11:59.602
And then you're going
to shift them around

00:11:59.602 --> 00:12:00.940
to whatever point you want.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:03.350
And many models use
this LayerNorm thing.

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:04.720
And it worked quite well.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:08.092
But many models have now
moved on to RMS Norm.

00:12:08.092 --> 00:12:09.800
And this is one of
the consensus changes.

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:13.420
Basically, all the models have
switched to using RMS Norm.

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:14.680
And now, what do you do, You?

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:16.728
Just drop the mean adjustment.

00:12:16.728 --> 00:12:18.020
So you don't subtract the mean.

00:12:18.020 --> 00:12:19.750
You don't add a bias term.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:21.350
And many notable models do this.

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:23.150
The LLaMA-family PaLM,
Chinchilla, T5's,

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:26.260
they've all moved to RMS Norm.

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:28.000
And what's the reason for this?

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.830
One reason is that it doesn't
really make a difference.

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:33.340
It turns out if you train
models with RMS Norm

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:35.950
does just as well as
training with LayerNorm.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:38.820
And so there's a
simplification argument.

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:40.830
But really, I think
the argument that's

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.470
often given in
these papers, and I

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:46.630
think it's good to appreciate
the details of this argument,

00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:51.880
is that going to RMS Norm is--
it's faster and just as good.

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:53.470
So in what way is it faster?

00:12:53.470 --> 00:12:56.470
Well, if I don't subtract the
mean, it's fewer operations.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:59.500
If I don't have to add
that bias term beta back,

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:02.490
it's fewer parameters that I
have to load from memory back

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:03.790
into my compute units.

00:13:03.790 --> 00:13:07.890
So I don't have to
retrieve this state.

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:10.150
And some of you might
be thinking, but wait,

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:13.530
you told me in 224N that nothing
but matrix multiplies matter

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:14.940
for the purpose of runtime.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.690
And this is not a
matrix multiply.

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:19.120
And so I shouldn't
care about any of this.

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:20.980
And that's a reasonable
perspective to take.

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:25.500
If you think about the number
of the percentage of flops that

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.840
is taken up by
different operations

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.270
in a transformer,
this table, there's

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:34.400
a nice paper by
Ivanov et al 2023.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:36.680
I think the title is Memory
Movement Is All You Need

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:40.490
or something that does profiling
of all the different components

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:42.090
of a transformer.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:43.860
And you see that
tensor contractions,

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.527
which are like matrix
multiplies, that's

00:13:45.527 --> 00:13:49.380
like 99.8% of the flops that
happen in a transformer.

00:13:49.380 --> 00:13:52.430
And so saving
0.17% of your flops

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:55.940
doesn't seem like a huge win.

00:13:55.940 --> 00:13:57.950
But I think one of the
things that's important

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:02.640
for architecture design now is
to not just think about flops.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:05.720
Because flops are important,
but that's not the only resource

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:07.610
that you have to think about.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:12.470
It's also that you have to think
carefully about memory movement.

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:15.360
And so even though
tensor contraction,

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:17.310
so this is things like
matrix multiplies.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:19.980
That's like 99.8% of the flops.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:23.880
If you have things like the
softmax operation or LayerNorms,

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.850
all these normalization
operations that

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:30.740
happen in a transformer,
there are 0.17% of the flops--

00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:33.500
actually, they're
25% of the runtime.

00:14:33.500 --> 00:14:37.150
And a big reason for that is
because these normalization

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:40.430
operations still incur a lot
of memory movement overhead.

00:14:40.430 --> 00:14:42.970
And so it does
actually matter to try

00:14:42.970 --> 00:14:46.040
to optimize some of
these lower level things

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:47.930
because it's not
just about flops,

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:49.370
it's also about memory movement.

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:51.790
I'm going to emphasize
this quite a bit more as I

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:53.060
get into the systems lecture.

00:14:53.060 --> 00:14:54.688
When we talk about
GPU architectures,

00:14:54.688 --> 00:14:56.230
it's going to become
very, very, very

00:14:56.230 --> 00:14:59.990
important to think about
memory, not just about flops.

00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:04.360
And so this is one of the
reasons why RMS Norm has now

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:07.430
become much more popular.

00:15:07.430 --> 00:15:11.050
And so I went back and looked
at some of the earlier RMS Norm

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:11.810
papers.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:15.130
I think the sad thing is
that there aren't quite

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:17.620
as many papers published
by industry labs

00:15:17.620 --> 00:15:19.360
with big, nice ablations.

00:15:19.360 --> 00:15:21.550
And so many of the
ablations that I'll show you

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:24.070
are going to be from a
couple of years back.

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:27.340
But Narang et al in 2020
had this very nice ablation

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:29.180
showing-- here's the
vanilla transformer,

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:30.790
here's the RMS Norm version.

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:33.150
And you see the exact
thing I told you.

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:35.640
The number of steps per second
that you can do in a vanilla

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:37.540
transformer, 3.5 per second.

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:39.660
With RMS Norm, you get 3.68.

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:42.640
Not a huge gain, but that's,
in some sense, for free.

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:47.340
And you get a final loss
that's lower than the vanilla

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:47.963
transformer.

00:15:47.963 --> 00:15:48.630
So that's great.

00:15:48.630 --> 00:15:51.510
In some sense, we've
gotten runtime improvements

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:53.580
and we've gotten
in fact, at least

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.540
in this case, loss improvements.

00:15:55.540 --> 00:15:59.670
And so that's a win-win for us.

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:03.570
The final thing that I'll say,
which is very much in line

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:06.250
with this RMS Norm
thing in terms of theme,

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:10.140
is that most modern transformers
do not have bias terms.

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:14.076
So the original transformer,
if you look at the FFN,

00:16:14.076 --> 00:16:15.430
will look something like this.

00:16:15.430 --> 00:16:16.540
You have your inputs x.

00:16:16.540 --> 00:16:18.670
You're going to do a linear
layer with a bias term.

00:16:18.670 --> 00:16:19.600
And then you ReLU it.

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:22.650
And then you'll have a second
linear layer wrapping around it.

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:26.852
But most implementations--
if they're not gated units,

00:16:26.852 --> 00:16:28.310
which I'll talk
about in a moment--

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:29.727
look actually
something like this.

00:16:29.727 --> 00:16:31.522
They just drop the bias terms.

00:16:31.522 --> 00:16:32.980
And you can just
make this argument

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:35.750
from basically the same kinds
of underlying principles.

00:16:35.750 --> 00:16:37.960
They perform just as well.

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:40.540
Matrix multiplies are
apparently all that you

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:42.430
need to get these guys to work.

00:16:42.430 --> 00:16:44.980
And the other thing,
which is maybe more subtle

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:48.460
is actually
optimization stability.

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:51.490
I don't quite have the deepest
understanding of why the bias

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:54.190
terms are particularly
bad for stability,

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:57.190
but there's been really
clear empirical observations

00:16:57.190 --> 00:16:59.800
that people have made that
basically dropping these bias

00:16:59.800 --> 00:17:02.980
terms often stabilizes the
training of these largest

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:03.830
neural networks.

00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.839
And so now, a lot of
the implementations

00:17:05.839 --> 00:17:09.369
now omit bias terms entirely and
train only on these pure matrix

00:17:09.369 --> 00:17:12.250
multiply kind of settings.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:16.089
So that's the LayerNorm bit.

00:17:16.089 --> 00:17:19.880
And so there is two things
that you should think of.

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:22.339
This is nice because the
story is pretty clear.

00:17:22.339 --> 00:17:25.130
Everyone does something, and
so you should just know this.

00:17:25.130 --> 00:17:26.640
Basically, everyone
does pre-norm.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:29.070
Or at least, they do
the LayerNorms outside

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:30.040
of the residual stream.

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:32.640
That's kind of the iron rule.

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:34.780
You get nicer
gradient propagation.

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:36.430
You get much more
stable training.

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:38.970
It just doesn't make sense
to do it the other way.

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:44.550
Most people or almost
everybody does RMS Norm.

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:47.010
In practice, it works almost
as well, has fewer parameters

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:47.860
to move around.

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:51.460
And this idea of dropping bias
terms just broadly applies.

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:55.270
A lot of these models just don't
have bias terms in most places.

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:57.550
I think the one exception
to this RMS Norm one,

00:17:57.550 --> 00:17:59.680
as I was reading
yesterday, is, I think,

00:17:59.680 --> 00:18:02.530
Cohere both Command A
and R+ use LayerNorm.

00:18:02.530 --> 00:18:05.460
Not quite sure why.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:11.490
Any questions on the LayerNorm,
RMS Norm and bias terms

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:12.760
stuff before I move on?

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:14.010
Yes, question?

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:17.130
Do you think there are some long
term lessons you can take away

00:18:17.130 --> 00:18:19.870
from these details that are
more future-proofed potentially,

00:18:19.870 --> 00:18:21.270
or do you think these are--

00:18:21.270 --> 00:18:22.410
yeah.

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:24.840
So the question was, is there
something more future-proof?

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:28.680
And I think it's hard to
have the biggest picture.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:32.450
In many ways, deep learning has
been very empirical and bottom

00:18:32.450 --> 00:18:33.540
up rather than top down.

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.320
But I do think there's some
generalizable lessons that you

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:37.410
could draw from here.

00:18:37.410 --> 00:18:41.060
I think the lesson of
have very direct identity

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:44.150
map residual connections is
a story and a lesson that

00:18:44.150 --> 00:18:46.070
has played out in many,
many different kinds

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:49.880
of architectures, not just
kinds of architectures.

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:51.620
The effectiveness
of LayerNorm we'll

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:54.350
see, once again, later
on in this lecture

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:55.650
has been very effective.

00:18:55.650 --> 00:18:58.700
And so not letting your
activations drift in scale

00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:00.380
is another thing that
I think generally

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:03.470
has been very effective
for training stability.

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:07.160
Those two seem like fairly
generalizable lessons.

00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:11.790
We will also see the systems
concerns come into play again.

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:13.430
So this is another
generalizable lesson

00:19:13.430 --> 00:19:15.590
of thinking really
carefully about the impact

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:17.450
of your architecture
on the systems

00:19:17.450 --> 00:19:21.190
components of your design.

00:19:21.190 --> 00:19:25.250
So now, there's this
other component,

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:26.690
which is the activations.

00:19:26.690 --> 00:19:29.800
And there is a whole
big zoo of activations--

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:32.840
ReLU, GeLU, Swiss, ELU, GeGLU.

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:34.820
And then, I mean, these
aren't activations.

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:36.325
There are different
kinds of MLPs--

00:19:36.325 --> 00:19:41.410
GeGLU, ReGLU, SeLU,
SwiGLU, and LiGLU.

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:45.160
And yeah, I think this
is exactly the kind thing

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:47.840
that I didn't originally
want to learn.

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:50.040
When I got into doing
deep learning, I was like,

00:19:50.040 --> 00:19:51.332
I don't care about activations.

00:19:51.332 --> 00:19:52.510
It's going to train anyway.

00:19:52.510 --> 00:19:54.260
But it really does matter.

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:56.600
Unfortunately, for
both you and me,

00:19:56.600 --> 00:19:59.530
that SwiGLU and
other GLU variants

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:01.040
just consistently work well.

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:02.500
And so I will
explain those to you.

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:04.750
And you should think about
them carefully because they

00:20:04.750 --> 00:20:06.500
do work and internalize that.

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:10.910
So I think the ReLU,
and maybe the GeLU,

00:20:10.910 --> 00:20:12.610
you all should already know.

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:15.820
The ReLU, you learn in some of
the most basic deep learning

00:20:15.820 --> 00:20:16.450
classes.

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:18.020
You just take the max of 0.

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:20.170
And in the case of the
MLP, you've got your--

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:22.970
I've dropped the bias
terms here. x dot W1,

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:25.730
you take the ReLU,
and then you do W2.

00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:27.340
Fairly easy.

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:31.120
A GeLU is a Gaussian
error linear unit.

00:20:31.120 --> 00:20:35.920
This one multiplies the linear
with a CDF of a Gaussian.

00:20:35.920 --> 00:20:39.100
And so it's basically
going to be like the ReLU,

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:41.360
but with a little
bit of a bump here.

00:20:41.360 --> 00:20:43.670
Hopefully, you can
see that over here.

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:45.880
This is not just flat
at the very bottom.

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:48.230
This makes things a little
bit more differentiable,

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:50.860
which may or may not help.

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:53.240
And the GPT family of models--

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:55.750
1, 2, 3, and GPTJ and so on--

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:58.540
all use the GeLU.

00:20:58.540 --> 00:21:01.090
And the original transformer
and some of the older models

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:02.690
used the ReLU.

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:05.230
And really, almost
all the modern models

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:08.320
have switched to the gated
linear units like SwiGLU

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:10.070
and the GeGLU and others.

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:15.190
And really, I think the Google
folks really pushed for this,

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:17.780
like PaLM, and T5 and others.

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:20.480
But since it's been tried
and true, basically,

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:27.140
almost all the models post
2023 use a gated linear unit.

00:21:27.140 --> 00:21:30.620
And so going back to that
earlier question of what

00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:32.630
generalizable
architecture things can

00:21:32.630 --> 00:21:34.050
we learn from this lecture?

00:21:34.050 --> 00:21:36.170
There are some things
that have really

00:21:36.170 --> 00:21:38.870
consistently been very
useful-- residual connections,

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:40.340
LayerNorms.

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:42.030
Gating is yet another one.

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:44.240
And so this is another
place where gating just

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:47.460
appears and is a very
good way of doing things.

00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:51.010
So, originally, this is
our fully connected layer,

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:51.510
right here.

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:52.770
This is with a ReLU.

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:56.130
Now, instead of doing
just linear and a ReLU,

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:59.870
what I'm going to do, is I'm
going to gate the output here

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:02.490
with entrywise linear term.

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:04.950
So x dot V is going
to give me a vector.

00:22:04.950 --> 00:22:07.700
And I'm going to
multiply that entrywise

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:11.450
with my original
inside term of the MLP.

00:22:11.450 --> 00:22:14.760
And then I'm going to multiply
the whole thing with W2.

00:22:14.760 --> 00:22:16.720
So the way to think
about this, is

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:20.200
I've gated the hidden
part of the MLP.

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:22.930
So I've got my original
activation that takes my inputs

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:24.980
and puts it into
the hidden space.

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:27.280
And then I'm going to
gate that with x dot V.

00:22:27.280 --> 00:22:30.070
And then I'm going
to project that back

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:33.260
into the hidden
dimensionality using W2.

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:36.260
So there's this gating operation
that happens entrywise.

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:38.990
And that's really the basic
thing that's happening here.

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:43.130
And this is the GLU plus
the ReLUs, the ReGLU.

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:44.590
And then we have
an extra parameter

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:46.220
that we've added
here for the gating.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:48.460
This is V.

00:22:48.460 --> 00:22:50.200
And so when someone
says something like,

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:53.950
oh, it's a GeGLU fully--

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:55.450
there's nothing to
laugh about that.

00:22:55.450 --> 00:22:58.360
There's the GeGLU
fully-connected layer,

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:03.680
what I've got here is, I've got
the GeLU for the non-linearity.

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:06.260
And I've still got the exact
same gating here of x dot V.

00:23:06.260 --> 00:23:08.410
And this is the architecture
that was used by many

00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:15.090
of the Google models like
T5v1.1, Gemma 2, Gemma 3.

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:18.460
And then another variants,
there's a SwiGLU,

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:20.170
and this has been
very, very popular.

00:23:20.170 --> 00:23:22.440
Swish is x times the sigmoid.

00:23:22.440 --> 00:23:23.950
And this is the non-linearity.

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:25.888
And sigmoid is like this.

00:23:25.888 --> 00:23:26.680
And x is like this.

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:30.090
So it will look just like
the Gaussian error unit.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:32.040
And then you do the
same thing here.

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:33.610
You have a gating
over the swish.

00:23:33.610 --> 00:23:35.890
And then you get a fully
connected layer here.

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:36.780
Yes?

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:37.650
I have a question.

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:41.670
Below a certain negative value,
the swish function and also

00:23:41.670 --> 00:23:45.902
the GeLU function, it's not
monotonically increasing.

00:23:45.902 --> 00:23:47.110
And in fact, it's decreasing.

00:23:47.110 --> 00:23:49.860
And a lot of the argument about
how gradient descent works

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:51.820
in introductory machine
learning is that, OK,

00:23:51.820 --> 00:23:54.570
you want to do gradient descent.

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:55.950
But here, it seems
like you would

00:23:55.950 --> 00:23:59.520
go in the opposite direction
if you use GeLU or swish

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:00.700
or their gated versions.

00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:02.230
So, I'm not--

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:06.370
So the question was, this
isn't monotonically decreasing.

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:09.150
There's a bit on the
very left of this 0 here

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:11.930
that's flipping
in the derivative.

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:14.390
And isn't that going
to be a problem?

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:16.732
I think intuitively,
you could have argued

00:24:16.732 --> 00:24:17.940
that this would be a problem.

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:20.930
You might trap a bunch
of activations at zeros.

00:24:20.930 --> 00:24:24.620
I think, in practice, if
you look at neural network

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:26.990
optimization dynamics,
what's actually

00:24:26.990 --> 00:24:29.810
happening is often you're
throwing very high learning

00:24:29.810 --> 00:24:32.310
rates with momentum
into the optimizer.

00:24:32.310 --> 00:24:36.000
And so you're not really going
to converge to the 0 point.

00:24:36.000 --> 00:24:38.990
These activations are going
to be all over the place.

00:24:38.990 --> 00:24:40.610
And so in practice,
I don't think

00:24:40.610 --> 00:24:43.700
this little tiny
negative piece is really

00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:46.250
an effect that's going
to be huge for the model,

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:47.230
if that makes sense.

00:24:49.940 --> 00:24:53.090
And then going back
to this, the SwiGLU

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:55.280
is basically most models today.

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:57.770
Like the LLaMA-family
PaLM, OlMo--

00:24:57.770 --> 00:24:59.910
and I'll show you
the big table later.

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:02.700
But you'll see that the
SwiGLU is very, very popular.

00:25:02.700 --> 00:25:03.950
And one thing to note--

00:25:03.950 --> 00:25:06.440
I'll talk about this again
in the hyperparameters part--

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:10.180
is now, remember, I've
added this V term,

00:25:10.180 --> 00:25:11.440
this extra parameter.

00:25:11.440 --> 00:25:15.130
And so I want to think
about how to size.

00:25:15.130 --> 00:25:16.420
This extra parameter.

00:25:16.420 --> 00:25:19.140
And what people do is
gated models usually

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:20.800
make this hidden size.

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:23.820
The basically output
dimensionality of W slightly

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:28.020
smaller by a factor of 2/3
in order to make sure that

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:31.590
the total number of parameters
of this whole thing remains

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.670
the same as the
non-gated counterparts.

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:36.150
And that's a convention
thing that most people do.

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:38.160
If you don't quite
understand what that is,

00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:40.527
I'll go back over
that again later.

00:25:40.527 --> 00:25:42.360
But you can just keep
in mind that basically

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:43.860
for the gated linear
units, you just

00:25:43.860 --> 00:25:45.390
make everything a
little bit smaller

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:50.130
to make sure things
remain parameter matched.

00:25:50.130 --> 00:25:52.930
So, yes, question.

00:25:52.930 --> 00:25:56.130
This may be obvious or
have gone over in the past.

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:59.340
One of the benefits
of ReLU is like, it's

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:02.140
very easily differentiable
by the input,

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:05.620
but if you have the derivative
of the CDF of the Gaussian,

00:26:05.620 --> 00:26:07.980
you have a squared with x.

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:11.180
Does that not really
slow things down?

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:12.780
That's a very good question.

00:26:12.780 --> 00:26:16.520
I'm not 100% sure what the
internal CUDA implementation

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:19.890
of the SwiGLU or the GeGLU is.

00:26:19.890 --> 00:26:22.670
I think it's entirely possible
that internally, they might be

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:25.040
implemented with lookup tables.

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:25.610
Go ahead.

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:28.140
I mean, what really matters
is the memory pressure here.

00:26:28.140 --> 00:26:29.750
And it will be the exact
same because you're reading

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:30.950
the same amount of elements.

00:26:30.950 --> 00:26:34.140
So the extra compute is
negligible on [INAUDIBLE].

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:36.770
That's probably
a better argument

00:26:36.770 --> 00:26:39.540
that basically flops why
this is negligible anyway.

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:44.305
And actually, the memory
calculus is the same, so.

00:26:44.305 --> 00:26:44.805
Cool.

00:26:47.520 --> 00:26:49.980
So, do gated linear units work?

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:52.263
I will have more modern
evidence for this as well.

00:26:52.263 --> 00:26:53.930
But I thought I should
take you straight

00:26:53.930 --> 00:26:58.370
to the horse's mouth, Noam
Shazeer's original paper, where

00:26:58.370 --> 00:27:01.760
he evaluates all
these GLU variants.

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:04.020
And this is somewhat
older stuff.

00:27:04.020 --> 00:27:06.910
So you're seeing CoLA
and SST-2 performance.

00:27:06.910 --> 00:27:11.050
But you do see basically that
the GLU variants consistently

00:27:11.050 --> 00:27:11.750
perform better.

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:17.590
GLU is 84.20,
84.12, 84.36, 84.67.

00:27:17.590 --> 00:27:19.550
And wow, it's 2020s.

00:27:19.550 --> 00:27:21.620
They even give you the
standard deviations

00:27:21.620 --> 00:27:24.530
so you can figure out how
significant those results are.

00:27:24.530 --> 00:27:26.450
And they, in fact,
are significant.

00:27:26.450 --> 00:27:31.090
And so this is some nice
evidence to see here.

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:34.270
There was also the Narang
et al in 2020 paper, which

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:36.910
is a very nice paper, studying
all sorts of architecture

00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:40.780
variants, I think in the
context of T5 style models.

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:43.840
And once again, you see
that the gated linear unit

00:27:43.840 --> 00:27:47.890
variants consistently
achieve lower losses

00:27:47.890 --> 00:27:48.980
than their counterparts.

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:53.230
You see that the bolded lines
are exactly at the GLU variants.

00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:57.640
And this pattern has
basically held up.

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.130
So for gating and
activations, there

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:03.960
are lots of variants
across different models.

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:06.060
But the gated linear
unit has become

00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:07.870
basically widespread
and dominant,

00:28:07.870 --> 00:28:09.720
and I think for good reason.

00:28:09.720 --> 00:28:13.930
Of course, the GLU isn't
necessary for a good model.

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:15.600
It's important to
separate the two.

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:18.420
Just because it's probably
the slightly better

00:28:18.420 --> 00:28:21.240
and everyone does it
doesn't mean it's necessary.

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:24.280
And you do see examples of
very high performance models.

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:25.050
not using a GLU.

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:27.490
Like GPT-3 is one example.

00:28:27.490 --> 00:28:32.110
A more recent one, Nemotron
340B uses a squared ReLU,

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:33.700
which I had not seen before.

00:28:33.700 --> 00:28:36.300
And Falcon 211b uses a ReLU.

00:28:36.300 --> 00:28:38.590
Both of those are relatively
high performance models.

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:41.230
So you can see that it's
not really necessary.

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:44.340
And so evidence does point
towards consistent gains

00:28:44.340 --> 00:28:46.060
from SwiGLU and GeGLU.

00:28:46.060 --> 00:28:50.455
And that's why we ask you to
implement exactly that variant.

00:28:50.455 --> 00:28:50.955
Cool.

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:57.580
The final thing that I want to
talk about for architectures,

00:28:57.580 --> 00:28:59.250
and this is one final--

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:02.540
major, I want to say,
variation that we've seen.

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:05.750
Normally, the transformer
block is serial.

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:09.720
In the sense that
for each block,

00:29:09.720 --> 00:29:12.360
the outputs come
in from the bottom.

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:14.750
And then you do your
attention, and then you

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:16.920
pass the result of that
computation forward.

00:29:16.920 --> 00:29:18.680
And then you do your
MLP, and then you

00:29:18.680 --> 00:29:21.035
pass that computation forward.

00:29:21.035 --> 00:29:22.410
And so this is
inherently serial.

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:24.350
You do attention, and then MLP.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:28.020
But of course, this might have
certain parallelism constraints.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:31.820
If you want to parallelize this
over gigantic sets of GPUs,

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:36.110
it might be harder to do so if
you have the serial connection.

00:29:36.110 --> 00:29:38.330
The systems concerns might
also be more difficult.

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:41.420
You might get lower
utilization from your GPUs.

00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:44.090
And so a few models
have done this thing

00:29:44.090 --> 00:29:46.910
that I'll call parallel layers.

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:50.450
Where, basically, instead
of having serial computation

00:29:50.450 --> 00:29:52.820
of attention and then
MLP, they will do them

00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:54.380
both at the same time.

00:29:54.380 --> 00:29:57.150
So you will get your x
from your previous layer.

00:29:57.150 --> 00:30:00.262
You will compute both the MLP
and the attention side by side,

00:30:00.262 --> 00:30:01.720
and then you will
add them together

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:03.013
into the residual stream.

00:30:03.013 --> 00:30:04.430
And then that will
be your output.

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:09.220
And this was pioneered by GPT-J,
which was this open source

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:10.430
replication effort.

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:13.270
And the folks at
Google doing PaLM

00:30:13.270 --> 00:30:16.840
were bold enough to do this
at the really big scale.

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:19.330
And many others
have followed since.

00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:21.080
So if you're
implementing this right,

00:30:21.080 --> 00:30:23.470
you can share a lot of
stuff like the Layer Norms.

00:30:23.470 --> 00:30:25.750
And the matrix multiplies
can get fused together.

00:30:25.750 --> 00:30:29.500
And you can get some systems
efficiencies out of that.

00:30:29.500 --> 00:30:31.690
It hasn't been quite as
popular since then, at least

00:30:31.690 --> 00:30:32.540
in the last year.

00:30:32.540 --> 00:30:34.082
I think most of the
models that we've

00:30:34.082 --> 00:30:36.582
seen have been serial layers
rather than parallel ones.

00:30:36.582 --> 00:30:39.040
I think the only exceptions to
this are like Cohere Command

00:30:39.040 --> 00:30:44.020
A, Command R+, and
a Falcon 2 11B.

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:48.670
So now, I think we have the
ability to go back to this big,

00:30:48.670 --> 00:30:51.280
hard to see chart, and
then see what I was

00:30:51.280 --> 00:30:53.180
pointing at the very beginning.

00:30:53.180 --> 00:30:55.343
So this column here,
you don't really

00:30:55.343 --> 00:30:57.010
need to be able to
read any of the text.

00:30:57.010 --> 00:30:59.135
Because I think the colors
will tell you everything

00:30:59.135 --> 00:30:59.870
you need to see.

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:03.200
This check mark here, this is
basically pre versus post-norm.

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:07.030
The only two models I really
know of in the early days that

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:10.090
did post norm, this is
the original transformer

00:31:10.090 --> 00:31:13.197
and GPT and BERT, if you want
to include that into this table.

00:31:13.197 --> 00:31:14.530
And then almost everybody else--

00:31:14.530 --> 00:31:18.080
I think, basically, everyone
else has done pre-norm.

00:31:18.080 --> 00:31:19.660
The only other non
checked boxes here

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:23.380
are models that are proprietary,
and I don't have details for.

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:25.940
This column here, on
the leftmost thing,

00:31:25.940 --> 00:31:27.860
this is RMS Norm
versus LayerNorm.

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:29.840
The gray boxes
are the LayerNorm.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:31.580
The blue ones are RMS Norm.

00:31:31.580 --> 00:31:34.960
Basically, most people have
converged to RMS Norm as I said.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:37.640
This column next to it is
serial and parallel layers.

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:39.350
Once again, most
people do serial,

00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:41.355
but you see other variants.

00:31:41.355 --> 00:31:42.730
What I'm going to
talk about next

00:31:42.730 --> 00:31:44.188
is going to be
position embeddings,

00:31:44.188 --> 00:31:46.327
and that will be more
interesting in a moment here.

00:31:46.327 --> 00:31:48.160
Any questions about any
of this architecture

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:49.603
stuff before I move on?

00:31:49.603 --> 00:31:51.520
Hopefully, that gives
you a bit of an overview

00:31:51.520 --> 00:31:56.720
of at least the major variations
in architectures that we see.

00:31:56.720 --> 00:32:00.080
Is the serial layer more
computationally efficient

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:01.040
than parallel layer?

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:03.620
So the question was
whether serial is

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:05.040
more efficient than parallel.

00:32:05.040 --> 00:32:06.890
It should be
actually the reverse.

00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:09.300
That parallel is more
efficient than serial.

00:32:09.300 --> 00:32:11.340
And that's why you're
willing to do this.

00:32:11.340 --> 00:32:13.190
So in some sense, you
might expect serial

00:32:13.190 --> 00:32:15.248
to be more expressive
because you're

00:32:15.248 --> 00:32:17.540
composing two computations
rather than just adding them

00:32:17.540 --> 00:32:18.530
together.

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:20.450
But the benefit of
parallel, in theory,

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:23.820
is that if you write the
right kinds of fused kernels,

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:25.950
a lot of these operations
can be done in parallel,

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:27.590
or the computation
is shared across

00:32:27.590 --> 00:32:31.490
the different parallel parts.

00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:34.550
So, cool.

00:32:34.550 --> 00:32:38.330
So the last thing I want to talk
about in architecture land--

00:32:38.330 --> 00:32:40.400
I think this is the last
thing-- is variations

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:42.223
in position embeddings.

00:32:42.223 --> 00:32:43.640
And I think this
one's interesting

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:47.940
because in the first
few years of LMs land,

00:32:47.940 --> 00:32:51.560
there were a lot of different
things that people were trying.

00:32:51.560 --> 00:32:54.470
Sign embeddings were from
the original transformer.

00:32:54.470 --> 00:32:56.440
You should have
learned this in 224N.

00:32:56.440 --> 00:32:58.660
There's sine and
cosine positions.

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:00.580
Many others did
absolute embeddings,

00:33:00.580 --> 00:33:03.370
like the GPTs and OPT,
all basically just

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:05.470
added a position
learned position vector

00:33:05.470 --> 00:33:06.880
to the embedding.

00:33:06.880 --> 00:33:11.530
Some others, like T5 and
Gopher, did various kinds

00:33:11.530 --> 00:33:13.510
of relative embeddings
that add vectors

00:33:13.510 --> 00:33:15.580
to the attention computation.

00:33:15.580 --> 00:33:18.040
And then, I think
most models have

00:33:18.040 --> 00:33:22.390
converged to rope, which is
relative position embeddings.

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:24.770
And this, I think,
actually started in GPTJ,

00:33:24.770 --> 00:33:27.250
once again, another open
source contribution.

00:33:27.250 --> 00:33:31.750
It has really rapidly been
picked up by most of the models.

00:33:31.750 --> 00:33:34.330
And so the high level
thought process behind RoPE

00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:38.380
is that the thing that
matters is relative positions

00:33:38.380 --> 00:33:39.710
of these vectors.

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:45.790
And so if I have an embedding f
of x of i, where x is the word

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:48.410
I'm trying to embed,
and i is my position,

00:33:48.410 --> 00:33:51.140
then I should be able to
write things down in this way.

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:55.055
So there should exist the f,
such that f of x, i, and f of y,

00:33:55.055 --> 00:33:57.820
j, if I take the inner
product of these embeddings,

00:33:57.820 --> 00:34:01.030
then, I can write this down
as some different function g,

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:03.120
which is a function
of the two words

00:34:03.120 --> 00:34:05.500
and the difference
in their positions.

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:11.280
So this is a definition
that enforces basically

00:34:11.280 --> 00:34:13.780
position invariance or
absolute position invariance.

00:34:13.780 --> 00:34:17.969
So you only pay attention to how
far apart these two words are.

00:34:17.969 --> 00:34:20.340
And so you can do a
brief check and see, OK,

00:34:20.340 --> 00:34:23.400
what happens with sines while
you get these cross terms that

00:34:23.400 --> 00:34:24.310
are not relative?

00:34:24.310 --> 00:34:27.480
So you do still leak absolute
position information.

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:29.730
Absolute positions
like it's in the name.

00:34:29.730 --> 00:34:32.159
It's not a relative
position embedding.

00:34:32.159 --> 00:34:36.060
And relative embeddings,
well, it is relative,

00:34:36.060 --> 00:34:37.659
but it's not an inner product.

00:34:37.659 --> 00:34:40.050
So it violates this constraint.

00:34:40.050 --> 00:34:43.800
And so RoPE is this kind
of clever observation

00:34:43.800 --> 00:34:46.980
that we do know
one thing, that is

00:34:46.980 --> 00:34:50.968
invariant to absolute
things, which is rotations.

00:34:50.968 --> 00:34:53.260
And so we're going to exploit
that structure to come up

00:34:53.260 --> 00:34:55.030
with our position embeddings.

00:34:55.030 --> 00:34:59.437
We know that inner products are
invariant to arbitrary rotation.

00:34:59.437 --> 00:35:00.770
So we're going to leverage that.

00:35:00.770 --> 00:35:03.170
So on the left, this
is the starting point.

00:35:03.170 --> 00:35:05.230
Let's say my embedding
for the word "we"

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:07.310
is this arrow over here.

00:35:07.310 --> 00:35:08.920
And my embedding
for the word "no"

00:35:08.920 --> 00:35:11.200
is this other arrow over here.

00:35:11.200 --> 00:35:14.350
Now, I want to embed this
sequence, "we know that."

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:16.760
And I look at the
word "we" and "no."

00:35:16.760 --> 00:35:17.630
So how do I do that?

00:35:17.630 --> 00:35:19.460
Well, we assume position 0.

00:35:19.460 --> 00:35:21.970
So I'm not going to
rotate that guy at all.

00:35:21.970 --> 00:35:27.400
"No" is in position one, so I'm
going to rotate him by one unit

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:28.310
of rotation.

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:31.490
And so now, I have this
embedding for "we know."

00:35:31.490 --> 00:35:34.700
And now, let's say I want to
embed this sequence, "Of course,

00:35:34.700 --> 00:35:35.330
we know."

00:35:35.330 --> 00:35:38.170
Now, "we" and "know" have
the same relative positioning

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:38.900
to each other.

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:40.640
And so let's look
at what happens.

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:42.550
We get shifted by two positions.

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:43.990
I rotate "we" by--

00:35:43.990 --> 00:35:46.300
I start in this
vertical position,

00:35:46.300 --> 00:35:48.490
and I rotate them
twice, 1 and 2.

00:35:48.490 --> 00:35:53.190
And then I rotate "no" by three
positions because it's 1, 2, 3--

00:35:53.190 --> 00:35:55.360
sorry, 0, 1, 2, 3rd position.

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:58.500
And so, now, if you look
at these two arrows,

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:00.190
they have the same
relative angle right.

00:36:00.190 --> 00:36:01.990
So their inner
products are preserved.

00:36:01.990 --> 00:36:04.930
And so this is the nice
fun idea about RoPE.

00:36:04.930 --> 00:36:07.030
You just rotate the vectors.

00:36:07.030 --> 00:36:10.470
And the rotation angle is
determined by the position

00:36:10.470 --> 00:36:12.900
of each word and rotations--

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:16.360
the inner products don't care
about relative rotations.

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:18.720
And so these inner
products are only

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:21.960
going to look at the
difference in distance.

00:36:21.960 --> 00:36:25.200
Now, it's easy to think about
in 2D because rotations are kind

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:26.940
of obvious in 2D.

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:29.340
There's only one way
to rotate a vector.

00:36:29.340 --> 00:36:32.050
But in high dimensional
spaces where we operate,

00:36:32.050 --> 00:36:35.620
it's not obvious at all how we
are going to do this rotation.

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:38.550
So the RoPE folks came
up with, in some ways,

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:41.410
the simplest but also
effective way of doing this.

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:44.280
And the way to do it is you take
your high dimensional vector.

00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:47.010
In this case, D. And I'm
just going to cut it up

00:36:47.010 --> 00:36:48.820
into blocks of two dimensions.

00:36:48.820 --> 00:36:52.750
And every two dimension is going
to be rotated by some theta.

00:36:52.750 --> 00:36:55.080
So there's going to
be a rotation speed.

00:36:55.080 --> 00:36:59.040
And I'm going to rotate
the pairs of dimensions.

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:01.170
And so now, every
pair of dimensions

00:37:01.170 --> 00:37:03.970
is encoding all of these
relative positions.

00:37:03.970 --> 00:37:06.310
And much like in sine
and cosine embeddings,

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:08.250
I'm going to pick
some set of thetas

00:37:08.250 --> 00:37:11.010
such that some embeddings
are rotated quickly,

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:13.570
and others are rotated
much more slowly.

00:37:13.570 --> 00:37:15.870
So they can capture both
high frequency information

00:37:15.870 --> 00:37:19.780
or close by information
and very far away,

00:37:19.780 --> 00:37:22.600
lower frequency
positioning information.

00:37:22.600 --> 00:37:27.222
And the actual
RoPE math here is,

00:37:27.222 --> 00:37:28.930
if you're going to
think about rotations,

00:37:28.930 --> 00:37:31.140
it's just going to be
multiplying with various sine

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:32.740
and cosine rotation matrices.

00:37:32.740 --> 00:37:36.150
Hopefully, you remember this
from linear algebra and trig.

00:37:36.150 --> 00:37:38.700
And so you can think
about this as an operation

00:37:38.700 --> 00:37:41.280
where you multiply
your embedding vectors

00:37:41.280 --> 00:37:45.610
with these block 2
by 2 block matrices.

00:37:45.610 --> 00:37:49.580
And there's no additive or
cross terms that appear here.

00:37:49.580 --> 00:37:53.950
This is all purely relative.

00:37:53.950 --> 00:37:58.150
One thing that is different, if
you're used to absolute position

00:37:58.150 --> 00:38:00.310
embeddings or sine
and cosine embeddings

00:38:00.310 --> 00:38:03.430
here is that the RoPE
is going to operate

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:04.870
at the actual attention layer.

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:07.400
You're not going to add position
embeddings at the bottom.

00:38:07.400 --> 00:38:08.950
Whenever these
attention computations

00:38:08.950 --> 00:38:10.367
are going to be
done, you're going

00:38:10.367 --> 00:38:11.900
to intervene on that layer.

00:38:11.900 --> 00:38:15.080
And then that's going to give
you your position information.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:17.830
And so I pulled this from I
think the LLaMA implementation

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:18.500
of RoPE.

00:38:18.500 --> 00:38:20.770
You've got the initial
normal attention stuff

00:38:20.770 --> 00:38:23.090
at the very top like
query keys and values.

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:25.930
These are your normal
linear projections.

00:38:25.930 --> 00:38:29.990
And then, you're going to come
up with cosine and sine angles.

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:31.990
These are rotation
angles telling you

00:38:31.990 --> 00:38:37.010
how much to rotate different
blocks of the query and key.

00:38:37.010 --> 00:38:39.340
And so you take your
query and your key,

00:38:39.340 --> 00:38:42.220
and you're going to rotate
them by the cosines and sines.

00:38:42.220 --> 00:38:44.652
And now, you've gotten
rotated query and rotated key.

00:38:44.652 --> 00:38:46.110
And that's going
to be what's going

00:38:46.110 --> 00:38:48.400
to go into the rest of
your attention computation.

00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:49.900
So you don't do
this at the bottom.

00:38:49.900 --> 00:38:52.570
You do it whenever you
generate your queries and keys.

00:38:52.570 --> 00:38:54.210
Hopefully that's clear.

00:38:54.210 --> 00:38:56.610
That's really
critical to enforcing

00:38:56.610 --> 00:39:00.900
this relative positioning
only information.

00:39:00.900 --> 00:39:01.800
OK?

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:04.440
Good.

00:39:04.440 --> 00:39:06.990
So one of the things
I want to highlight

00:39:06.990 --> 00:39:09.360
is that RoPE is actually
one of the things

00:39:09.360 --> 00:39:11.680
that it seems like
everyone has converged on.

00:39:11.680 --> 00:39:15.610
I went through all 19 of
those papers over the weekend.

00:39:15.610 --> 00:39:18.210
And basically, all
of them now use RoPE

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:20.520
for various different reasons.

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:23.340
The reason that RoPE has now
many different algorithms

00:39:23.340 --> 00:39:25.120
for extrapolating
context length,

00:39:25.120 --> 00:39:27.840
and that's an important part
of the modern productionized

00:39:27.840 --> 00:39:29.400
language model.

00:39:29.400 --> 00:39:31.930
But also, it seems to be
empirically quite effective,

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:34.480
even at fairly small scales
and small context lengths.

00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:37.080
So it's kind of
worn out on this--

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:40.790
what's it called--
position embedding battle.

00:39:40.790 --> 00:39:43.460
Any questions before I move on
to some of the hyperparameter

00:39:43.460 --> 00:39:43.710
stuff?

00:39:43.710 --> 00:39:44.210
Yes.

00:39:44.210 --> 00:39:46.370
Is the rate of rotation
is consistent across all

00:39:46.370 --> 00:39:47.712
of these models?

00:39:47.712 --> 00:39:49.170
I don't think
they're all the same.

00:39:49.170 --> 00:39:50.712
There's some variation
in the thetas.

00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:54.150
Yes.

00:39:54.150 --> 00:39:59.690
Are the thetas for each pair,
are those hyperparameters

00:39:59.690 --> 00:40:01.580
or are they trained?

00:40:01.580 --> 00:40:04.133
The thetas that determine
the rotation angles,

00:40:04.133 --> 00:40:05.300
they're not hyperparameters.

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:09.600
Much like in the sines
and cosines here,

00:40:09.600 --> 00:40:12.320
there's a schedule to
the rotation angles

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:13.290
that are determined.

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:16.020
And it's in the same intuition
as the sines and cosines.

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:18.650
You want to cover different
frequency ranges in order

00:40:18.650 --> 00:40:24.020
to get higher or lower
frequency information.

00:40:24.020 --> 00:40:24.980
Yes.

00:40:24.980 --> 00:40:28.050
The rotations create any
difficulty with training,

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:31.430
I wonder like this,
angular rotations.

00:40:31.430 --> 00:40:33.990
The rotations themselves don't
really create any issues.

00:40:33.990 --> 00:40:35.540
Because one way of
thinking about a rotation

00:40:35.540 --> 00:40:37.040
is that it's just
a matrix multiply.

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:40.940
Since thetas are fixed and
the m's here are fixed,

00:40:40.940 --> 00:40:42.790
this is really
just a fixed matrix

00:40:42.790 --> 00:40:44.270
that multiplies your vector.

00:40:44.270 --> 00:40:46.340
And so in that sense,
it's not really an issue.

00:40:46.340 --> 00:40:47.620
If you were learning
the thetas, then maybe

00:40:47.620 --> 00:40:49.787
you have issues because
you're maybe differentiating

00:40:49.787 --> 00:40:50.780
through trig functions.

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:53.860
But you're not
doing that here, so.

00:40:53.860 --> 00:40:56.410
OK, cool.

00:40:56.410 --> 00:41:00.310
So now, I think we go even one
more level into the details

00:41:00.310 --> 00:41:00.920
here.

00:41:00.920 --> 00:41:03.610
And we're going to talk
about hyperparameters.

00:41:03.610 --> 00:41:06.280
I feel like when you have
to-- you're dropped in,

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:09.363
and you're asked to train
a new language model.

00:41:09.363 --> 00:41:11.780
There's a lot of questions you
have about hyperparameters,

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:13.343
because there's
quite a few of them.

00:41:13.343 --> 00:41:15.010
And one of the things
that I've realized

00:41:15.010 --> 00:41:16.690
is that actually,
only a few of these

00:41:16.690 --> 00:41:19.868
really get changed across
different successful models.

00:41:19.868 --> 00:41:21.910
There's actually like
fairly clear rules of thumb

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:25.660
and fairly clear guidelines that
people seem to be following.

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:28.330
So there are some things
like how much bigger should

00:41:28.330 --> 00:41:29.620
the feed-forward size be?

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:31.460
Or how many heads should I have?

00:41:31.460 --> 00:41:33.820
Or what should my vocab size be?

00:41:33.820 --> 00:41:36.040
And so we'll talk about
each of those things,

00:41:36.040 --> 00:41:38.880
and we'll try to constrain
the space of hyperparameters

00:41:38.880 --> 00:41:41.460
that people have.

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:43.920
So the starting
point, we're going

00:41:43.920 --> 00:41:46.030
to look at a simple
feed-forward layer.

00:41:46.030 --> 00:41:48.810
Just with the bias, let's say.

00:41:48.810 --> 00:41:50.470
This is a ReLU version of it.

00:41:50.470 --> 00:41:52.930
And so there's two
hyperparameters here.

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:55.480
There's d model, which is
the dimensionality of x.

00:41:55.480 --> 00:41:58.260
That's the input
coming into your MLP.

00:41:58.260 --> 00:41:59.807
And then you've got dff.

00:41:59.807 --> 00:42:01.390
So this is the
feed-forward dimension.

00:42:01.390 --> 00:42:05.170
This is the output hidden
dimension of your MLP.

00:42:05.170 --> 00:42:07.900
And from there, you're going
to project back onto d model.

00:42:07.900 --> 00:42:10.860
So what should dff be?

00:42:10.860 --> 00:42:12.300
In general.

00:42:12.300 --> 00:42:15.160
These things are going
to be up projections.

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:18.510
You're going to have more hidden
units than there were inputs.

00:42:18.510 --> 00:42:19.660
But how much bigger?

00:42:19.660 --> 00:42:22.620
Well, there is actually
just like a consensus.

00:42:22.620 --> 00:42:28.560
Almost everybody that uses ReLU
style MLPs are going to pick dff

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:32.760
is equal to 4 times d model.

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:35.310
I will show you some
empirical evidence for why

00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:37.470
this is a sane number later.

00:42:37.470 --> 00:42:39.570
But as far as I
can tell, there's

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:43.020
no law of nature that
says you have to pick 4.

00:42:43.020 --> 00:42:45.430
This is a convention
that has really held up.

00:42:45.430 --> 00:42:49.500
Now, there are a few
exceptions to this rule.

00:42:49.500 --> 00:42:53.280
Remember that the GLU variants
are going to scale this down

00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:54.700
by a factor of 2/3.

00:42:54.700 --> 00:42:58.050
And if you scale it
down by a factor of 2/3,

00:42:58.050 --> 00:43:02.220
you're going to have roughly
the same number of parameters.

00:43:02.220 --> 00:43:03.580
You can do a little bit of math.

00:43:03.580 --> 00:43:06.480
And if you scale the GLU
variants down by a factor

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:09.270
of 2/3, you'll come to the
conclusion that the way to do

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:12.788
that is to set dff equal
to 8 over 3d model.

00:43:12.788 --> 00:43:14.830
That's going to be the
number that you end up at.

00:43:14.830 --> 00:43:16.710
And you guys convince yourself
that that will give you

00:43:16.710 --> 00:43:18.190
the same number of parameters.

00:43:18.190 --> 00:43:20.107
And that's the ratio
that you would get if you

00:43:20.107 --> 00:43:22.440
started with a ratio of 4.

00:43:22.440 --> 00:43:24.400
So if you look at
many of the models,

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:26.850
they actually do follow
this rule of thumb.

00:43:26.850 --> 00:43:29.910
PaLM, for example--

00:43:29.910 --> 00:43:31.910
PaLM, Mistral, and LLaMA
are slightly larger.

00:43:31.910 --> 00:43:34.940
These are GLU models, but they
don't follow this 2.6 rule.

00:43:34.940 --> 00:43:38.110
But if you look at, for example,
LLaMA-1, Qwen, DeepSeek,

00:43:38.110 --> 00:43:43.810
Yi, and T5, they all roughly
follow this 2.6 ish rule.

00:43:43.810 --> 00:43:46.960
And I can put up
the big table of LMs

00:43:46.960 --> 00:43:48.770
that I made later
with hyperparameters.

00:43:48.770 --> 00:43:52.730
Many, many, many of them fall
into this roughly 2.6 range.

00:43:52.730 --> 00:43:58.240
And that's the standard
parameterization of a GLU unit.

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:00.110
I'll go through one
other exception.

00:44:00.110 --> 00:44:04.540
I really like this exception
because I think, in many ways,

00:44:04.540 --> 00:44:07.240
big large language
model training is

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:08.980
a game of copying
hyperparameters

00:44:08.980 --> 00:44:09.817
from other people.

00:44:09.817 --> 00:44:11.150
And so we don't learn very much.

00:44:11.150 --> 00:44:13.000
It's very conservative.

00:44:13.000 --> 00:44:15.520
But T5, I really like.

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:17.463
Because in some sense,
it's really bold.

00:44:17.463 --> 00:44:18.880
And I think Google
people actually

00:44:18.880 --> 00:44:20.830
do some pretty bold stuff.

00:44:20.830 --> 00:44:25.040
And so if you look at the 11
billion parameter T5 model,

00:44:25.040 --> 00:44:27.860
they have a pretty
incredible setting.

00:44:27.860 --> 00:44:30.240
Their hidden dim is 1,024.

00:44:30.240 --> 00:44:33.770
But their dff, their
up-projected dimension is

00:44:33.770 --> 00:44:35.040
65,000.

00:44:35.040 --> 00:44:38.240
And so that's going
to give you a 64 times

00:44:38.240 --> 00:44:42.540
multiplier on the ratio
of dff to d model.

00:44:42.540 --> 00:44:44.600
And of course, you
compare to this, where

00:44:44.600 --> 00:44:45.770
PaLM is like a factor of 4.

00:44:45.770 --> 00:44:47.550
And everyone else
is much smaller.

00:44:47.550 --> 00:44:50.570
This is a very large difference.

00:44:50.570 --> 00:44:52.250
And there's some
other recent examples

00:44:52.250 --> 00:44:55.430
of using much
bigger multipliers.

00:44:55.430 --> 00:44:57.830
Gemma 2 follows
in these footsteps

00:44:57.830 --> 00:44:59.280
and does a factor of 8.

00:44:59.280 --> 00:45:01.950
And I'll talk a little bit
about this exception later.

00:45:01.950 --> 00:45:03.900
Of course, T5 was a
totally fine model.

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:06.290
So this should tell you
it is possible to train

00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:11.090
a model with such a
much larger ratio.

00:45:11.090 --> 00:45:14.270
So one of the things that I
think is quantitative evidence,

00:45:14.270 --> 00:45:15.830
I saw that 4x multiplier.

00:45:15.830 --> 00:45:19.020
And I thought, is that
really the right thing to do,

00:45:19.020 --> 00:45:21.020
or is there some more
quantitative experiment

00:45:21.020 --> 00:45:24.470
someone's done to convince
me that that is a good idea?

00:45:24.470 --> 00:45:28.595
So one of the figures from
Jared Kaplan's scaling law

00:45:28.595 --> 00:45:30.220
paper-- and most
people know this paper

00:45:30.220 --> 00:45:32.030
for the scaling law component.

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:34.660
But actually, there's also some
really useful hyperparameter

00:45:34.660 --> 00:45:36.340
components to this paper.

00:45:36.340 --> 00:45:38.800
You'll actually see that
they do exactly this thing

00:45:38.800 --> 00:45:42.010
that I'm talking about,
the dff to d model ratio.

00:45:42.010 --> 00:45:44.320
And they plot essentially
how much the loss

00:45:44.320 --> 00:45:46.300
increases as you vary this.

00:45:46.300 --> 00:45:50.360
And you see that
there's a sweet spot.

00:45:50.360 --> 00:45:55.670
This is a ratio of 1, 2, 3, 4
and then up to 10 or so here.

00:45:55.670 --> 00:45:58.630
And so there's a
pretty wide basin here,

00:45:58.630 --> 00:46:01.730
anywhere between 1
to maybe up to 10,

00:46:01.730 --> 00:46:05.480
where you can pick whatever
feed-forward ratio you want.

00:46:05.480 --> 00:46:08.020
And it'll be roughly optimal.

00:46:08.020 --> 00:46:12.410
And 4 is not too far off from
your optimal choices over here.

00:46:12.410 --> 00:46:14.662
It's 1, 2, 3, 4.

00:46:14.662 --> 00:46:16.370
It's like right here
or maybe right here.

00:46:16.370 --> 00:46:18.210
So that's a pretty
reasonable choice.

00:46:21.400 --> 00:46:24.950
So what can we learn from all
of this hyperparameter stuff?

00:46:24.950 --> 00:46:26.940
I think a lot of the
evidence points towards--

00:46:26.940 --> 00:46:30.700
you can pick the same defaults
of, if you're not using a glue,

00:46:30.700 --> 00:46:31.900
you can multiply by 4.

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:35.700
If you're using a glue,
you can use roughly 2.66.

00:46:35.700 --> 00:46:40.110
And they can work pretty well
for mostly all the modern LMs.

00:46:40.110 --> 00:46:42.630
T5, once again, does
show that you don't

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:43.950
have to follow these rules.

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:46.710
You can be a rule breaker
and do whatever you'd like.

00:46:46.710 --> 00:46:48.880
There's no hyperparameter
choice written in stone.

00:46:48.880 --> 00:46:52.290
You can get reasonable LMs at
many other hyperparameters.

00:46:52.290 --> 00:46:55.890
That said, I think the really
funny epilogue to this story is

00:46:55.890 --> 00:47:01.150
that T5 has a follow up model
called T5v1.1 that's improved.

00:47:01.150 --> 00:47:04.420
And it uses a much more standard
2.5 multiplier on GeGLU.

00:47:04.420 --> 00:47:06.420
So you can read
between the lines

00:47:06.420 --> 00:47:09.550
and say maybe they looked
at the original T5 and said,

00:47:09.550 --> 00:47:13.020
actually, maybe we want to walk
back that 64 times multiplier

00:47:13.020 --> 00:47:14.423
and pick a more standard one.

00:47:14.423 --> 00:47:16.090
And they did end up
with a better model.

00:47:16.090 --> 00:47:17.865
So, cool.

00:47:17.865 --> 00:47:20.280
What is the relationship
between the ratio

00:47:20.280 --> 00:47:26.190
and the model efficiency
on [INAUDIBLE]?

00:47:26.190 --> 00:47:28.020
So I think that's
a good question.

00:47:28.020 --> 00:47:30.505
So the question was,
what's the ratio--

00:47:30.505 --> 00:47:31.880
or sorry, what's
the relationship

00:47:31.880 --> 00:47:35.480
between this ratio that I'm
talking about here and generally

00:47:35.480 --> 00:47:36.480
the impact on the model?

00:47:36.480 --> 00:47:43.520
And so if we go all
the way back here,

00:47:43.520 --> 00:47:47.120
the ratio is controlling
essentially how wide

00:47:47.120 --> 00:47:49.830
the hidden part of this MLP is.

00:47:49.830 --> 00:47:51.770
And so the original
justification

00:47:51.770 --> 00:47:54.980
in the T5 paper for
picking 64 was to say,

00:47:54.980 --> 00:47:58.220
actually, we can get bigger
and fatter matrix multiplies

00:47:58.220 --> 00:48:00.540
if we make that dimension
really, really large.

00:48:00.540 --> 00:48:03.980
And while that is kind of a
true statement, the wider it is,

00:48:03.980 --> 00:48:07.560
you're getting more parallel
computation, so to speak,

00:48:07.560 --> 00:48:08.880
rather than serial computation.

00:48:08.880 --> 00:48:10.730
So you're spending your
flops and your parameters

00:48:10.730 --> 00:48:13.313
in a slightly different way than
if you made your hidden units

00:48:13.313 --> 00:48:15.450
bigger, which would let
you pass more information,

00:48:15.450 --> 00:48:17.690
or using more units,
which would give you

00:48:17.690 --> 00:48:19.350
more serial computation.

00:48:19.350 --> 00:48:21.440
So you're spending your
parameters and your flops

00:48:21.440 --> 00:48:24.750
in a slightly suboptimal
way from expressive power,

00:48:24.750 --> 00:48:29.120
but you might get systems
gains if your matrices are

00:48:29.120 --> 00:48:29.635
wide enough.

00:48:33.260 --> 00:48:35.750
Excellent.

00:48:35.750 --> 00:48:38.960
So another thing that is
a surprising-- or maybe

00:48:38.960 --> 00:48:41.750
not surprising--
consensus hyperparameter

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:45.620
is the ratio between
the model dimension

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:48.530
and the head dimension
times the number of heads.

00:48:48.530 --> 00:48:51.200
So I clipped this from 224N.

00:48:51.200 --> 00:48:54.860
But really, the basically
canonical choice

00:48:54.860 --> 00:48:57.890
is to pick things so
that the dimension d--

00:48:57.890 --> 00:49:01.200
that's a hidden dimension--
and if you have multiple heads,

00:49:01.200 --> 00:49:03.870
you're just going to split
up the number of dimensions

00:49:03.870 --> 00:49:04.890
each head gets.

00:49:04.890 --> 00:49:07.070
So you're going to keep
the dimensions fixed

00:49:07.070 --> 00:49:08.730
as you add more heads.

00:49:08.730 --> 00:49:10.088
And you don't have to do that.

00:49:10.088 --> 00:49:11.630
As you add more
heads, you could just

00:49:11.630 --> 00:49:13.612
keep the same number
of dimensions per head,

00:49:13.612 --> 00:49:15.320
and you could just
let the attention part

00:49:15.320 --> 00:49:16.770
take more and more parameters.

00:49:16.770 --> 00:49:18.000
You could do that.

00:49:18.000 --> 00:49:19.620
That's an option that you have.

00:49:19.620 --> 00:49:23.190
But most models, once again,
do follow this guideline.

00:49:23.190 --> 00:49:26.620
We see GPT-3, T5,
LaMDA, PaLM, and LLaMA2,

00:49:26.620 --> 00:49:30.570
they all have a ratio of
one or almost exactly one.

00:49:30.570 --> 00:49:33.490
T5 is the one exception
that breaks this rule.

00:49:33.490 --> 00:49:36.660
They tried the big ratio of 16.

00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:41.050
But otherwise, it is all fairly
following this consensus.

00:49:41.050 --> 00:49:43.260
There's been a couple of
papers that have argued

00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:45.720
against this 1 to 1 ratio.

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:47.290
There's a notable one by--

00:49:47.290 --> 00:49:48.790
I don't know how
to pronounce this--

00:49:48.790 --> 00:49:51.420
Bhojanapalli et
al 2020, who have

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:54.850
argued that if you have
more and more heads,

00:49:54.850 --> 00:49:57.270
they're going to have
lower and lower rank.

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:59.620
And if you have very
few dimensions per head,

00:49:59.620 --> 00:50:01.710
that's going to start
affecting the expressiveness

00:50:01.710 --> 00:50:03.540
of the attention operation.

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:05.310
But in practice,
it doesn't really

00:50:05.310 --> 00:50:08.010
seem like we see too
many significant low rank

00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:09.660
bottlenecks in practice.

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:13.450
And most of the models with this
ratio of 1 seem to do just fine.

00:50:13.450 --> 00:50:15.120
This is really a
parameter that's

00:50:15.120 --> 00:50:18.320
generally been held constant
by most of the models

00:50:18.320 --> 00:50:19.137
that we've seen.

00:50:19.137 --> 00:50:20.720
If I have time, I'll
talk a little bit

00:50:20.720 --> 00:50:23.210
about different optimizations
that people have made

00:50:23.210 --> 00:50:25.520
on this multi-head component.

00:50:25.520 --> 00:50:30.710
But hyperparameter wise, things
have stayed fairly similar.

00:50:30.710 --> 00:50:34.850
I think one of the big ones
in terms of hyperparameters

00:50:34.850 --> 00:50:37.100
is the aspect ratio.

00:50:37.100 --> 00:50:39.680
So we can think
about deep networks.

00:50:39.680 --> 00:50:41.090
We can have more
and more layers,

00:50:41.090 --> 00:50:43.200
or we can have wide networks.

00:50:43.200 --> 00:50:45.600
And generally, if you want
one knob to control the width,

00:50:45.600 --> 00:50:48.260
that would be the
hidden dimension

00:50:48.260 --> 00:50:49.370
of the residual stream.

00:50:49.370 --> 00:50:51.037
That would control
essentially the width

00:50:51.037 --> 00:50:53.340
of almost all the
operations at once.

00:50:53.340 --> 00:50:57.033
And so this seems like a
pretty critical thing to tune.

00:50:57.033 --> 00:50:58.700
You might think that
deeper networks are

00:50:58.700 --> 00:51:00.620
smarter and more expressive,
or wider networks

00:51:00.620 --> 00:51:02.300
are more efficient.

00:51:02.300 --> 00:51:05.360
There is generally a
sweet spot of ratios

00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:07.160
that people have picked.

00:51:07.160 --> 00:51:09.000
There have been outliers.

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:12.690
Some of the early models used
much smaller ratios here.

00:51:12.690 --> 00:51:16.720
So what that means
is that they were

00:51:16.720 --> 00:51:18.980
much wider than they were deep.

00:51:18.980 --> 00:51:21.880
And then some models
have gone really deep

00:51:21.880 --> 00:51:25.300
where they had way more-- sorry,
the other way around-- really

00:51:25.300 --> 00:51:28.760
wide, where they had way
more d model than n layer.

00:51:28.760 --> 00:51:31.250
And there's been generally
a sweet spot of saying,

00:51:31.250 --> 00:51:35.710
we want about 128 hidden
dimensions per layer.

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:38.380
And that has been
generally stuck to

00:51:38.380 --> 00:51:42.010
by a lot of the GPT-3
and LLaMA variant models.

00:51:42.010 --> 00:51:46.720
And I'll talk a little bit about
evidence for that in a second.

00:51:46.720 --> 00:51:49.060
There's considerations
about aspect ratio

00:51:49.060 --> 00:51:50.920
that are quite important.

00:51:50.920 --> 00:51:55.340
They will control the amount
of parallelism that we can do.

00:51:55.340 --> 00:51:59.015
So if you're doing something
called pipeline parallel,

00:51:59.015 --> 00:52:00.640
what you're often
going to do is you're

00:52:00.640 --> 00:52:02.260
going to take your
different layers

00:52:02.260 --> 00:52:03.700
and you're going to
cut them up and you're

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:05.950
going to put them on different
devices or different blocks

00:52:05.950 --> 00:52:08.380
of devices because you'll
parallelize within each layer

00:52:08.380 --> 00:52:09.550
as well.

00:52:09.550 --> 00:52:13.950
And so there's going to be
certain kinds of constraints

00:52:13.950 --> 00:52:15.760
that you're going to
put on your model.

00:52:15.760 --> 00:52:18.060
And also, if you have
really wide models,

00:52:18.060 --> 00:52:20.310
then you can do something
called tensor parallel where

00:52:20.310 --> 00:52:22.320
you slice up the
matrices, and then you

00:52:22.320 --> 00:52:24.010
distribute those on GPUs.

00:52:24.010 --> 00:52:27.870
And one thing that we'll learn
in, I think, 1, 2, 3, 4 or 5

00:52:27.870 --> 00:52:31.530
lectures is that these different
parallelism paradigms are going

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:33.030
to have different constraints.

00:52:33.030 --> 00:52:36.520
You need really fast
networking for tensor parallel.

00:52:36.520 --> 00:52:40.140
And you can maybe get away with
slower networking or higher

00:52:40.140 --> 00:52:42.460
latency networking
for pipeline parallel.

00:52:42.460 --> 00:52:44.280
And so your
networking constraints

00:52:44.280 --> 00:52:47.580
might, in turn, drive
some of these width-depth

00:52:47.580 --> 00:52:48.760
considerations.

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:50.940
But setting that aside,
you might abstractly

00:52:50.940 --> 00:52:54.620
ask, what is the impact
of aspect ratio on model

00:52:54.620 --> 00:52:55.120
performance?

00:52:55.120 --> 00:53:00.570
And once again, Kaplan et al
have a really nice visual aid

00:53:00.570 --> 00:53:03.475
showing how aspect ratio
impacts performance.

00:53:03.475 --> 00:53:05.100
And so this is three
different scales--

00:53:05.100 --> 00:53:09.580
50 million, 274 million,
and 1.5 billion parameters.

00:53:09.580 --> 00:53:12.290
And the x-axis is aspect ratio.

00:53:12.290 --> 00:53:16.490
Y-axis is loss difference
in percentage change.

00:53:16.490 --> 00:53:20.360
And you see, that around
100, which is, once again, I

00:53:20.360 --> 00:53:22.200
told you was around
the consensus

00:53:22.200 --> 00:53:24.170
choice of hyperparameters,
is the minimum

00:53:24.170 --> 00:53:25.410
across different scales.

00:53:25.410 --> 00:53:30.470
So this is backed by some of
this large scale hyperparameter

00:53:30.470 --> 00:53:32.700
data that's been
published by Kaplan et al

00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:34.410
and roughly matches
that intuition.

00:53:34.410 --> 00:53:36.050
And a really nice
thing here, is it

00:53:36.050 --> 00:53:39.290
seems to be the case that
aspect ratio optima does not

00:53:39.290 --> 00:53:43.560
shift too much across several
orders of magnitude here.

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:45.990
So if this holds up even
more, that's very good news.

00:53:45.990 --> 00:53:50.690
You can keep training on
one fixed aspect ratio.

00:53:50.690 --> 00:53:55.250
One thing I will know that is
quite an interesting result is

00:53:55.250 --> 00:53:59.600
E Tay and others at Google had
this very interesting paper

00:53:59.600 --> 00:54:02.780
studying the impact
of depth versus width

00:54:02.780 --> 00:54:04.650
both upstream and downstream.

00:54:04.650 --> 00:54:06.320
And one of the things
that they found

00:54:06.320 --> 00:54:10.230
was that if you're
looking at losses, then,

00:54:10.230 --> 00:54:11.430
it doesn't really matter.

00:54:11.430 --> 00:54:13.240
Parameter is the only
thing that matters.

00:54:13.240 --> 00:54:15.210
Deeper models don't help you.

00:54:15.210 --> 00:54:17.460
But the story is
less clear if you're

00:54:17.460 --> 00:54:18.837
looking at downstream accuracy.

00:54:18.837 --> 00:54:20.670
At the time, they were
looking at fine tuned

00:54:20.670 --> 00:54:21.940
superglue accuracy.

00:54:21.940 --> 00:54:24.310
They were arguing that for
the same amount of flops,

00:54:24.310 --> 00:54:25.570
deeper models might be better.

00:54:25.570 --> 00:54:27.580
So I'll just leave it at that.

00:54:27.580 --> 00:54:29.818
There's not quite as much
follow up to this work,

00:54:29.818 --> 00:54:31.360
at least in the
open, that I've seen.

00:54:31.360 --> 00:54:33.330
But downstream
performance may actually

00:54:33.330 --> 00:54:35.880
be slightly different in
terms of the aspect ratio

00:54:35.880 --> 00:54:36.785
considerations here.

00:54:41.850 --> 00:54:43.650
Cool.

00:54:43.650 --> 00:54:46.290
The final thing that
I want to talk about

00:54:46.290 --> 00:54:48.870
in this very low level
hyperparameter world

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:53.010
is what are the vocabulary sizes
that you might want to pick.

00:54:53.010 --> 00:54:57.510
And in general, vocabulary sizes
have been trending upwards.

00:54:57.510 --> 00:55:01.350
And I think a big part of
why is because LLMs are

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:02.830
being deployed out in the wild.

00:55:02.830 --> 00:55:04.630
They're becoming
more useful services.

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:06.088
And when that
happens, you're going

00:55:06.088 --> 00:55:08.690
to interact with people speaking
different languages, people

00:55:08.690 --> 00:55:12.860
using emojis, all sorts of
other kinds of almost modalities

00:55:12.860 --> 00:55:15.860
or languages than
what you might expect.

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:18.600
And so I think some
of the earlier models,

00:55:18.600 --> 00:55:20.810
and especially
monolingual models,

00:55:20.810 --> 00:55:25.950
ranged around in the 30,000 to
50,000 token vocabulary range.

00:55:25.950 --> 00:55:29.210
And you can see this in
GPTs, the early LLaMAs.

00:55:29.210 --> 00:55:31.400
But if you look at
the multilingual,

00:55:31.400 --> 00:55:34.920
or I would call production
systems that have come out,

00:55:34.920 --> 00:55:39.110
they've all been shifting
towards the 100,000 to 250,000

00:55:39.110 --> 00:55:41.900
range for their
vocabulary sizes.

00:55:41.900 --> 00:55:45.625
And I looked at Command A,
which is one of Cahere's models.

00:55:45.625 --> 00:55:47.000
They're a company
that emphasizes

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:48.540
a lot of multilingual stuff.

00:55:48.540 --> 00:55:52.220
You see very large
vocab sizes from them.

00:55:52.220 --> 00:55:56.060
Even with GPT-4 and many others
that have copied the GPT-4

00:55:56.060 --> 00:55:59.150
tokenizer are going to
be around 100k tokens.

00:55:59.150 --> 00:56:03.020
And so that's the standard that
a lot of people are operating

00:56:03.020 --> 00:56:07.640
at, roughly at 100k
to 200k token size.

00:56:07.640 --> 00:56:09.190
And I think there's
been work showing

00:56:09.190 --> 00:56:12.160
that as models get bigger,
these models can, in some sense,

00:56:12.160 --> 00:56:17.020
handle more and more, or make
good use of more and more vocab

00:56:17.020 --> 00:56:17.660
elements.

00:56:17.660 --> 00:56:21.040
And so you might see increasing
trends to token counts

00:56:21.040 --> 00:56:23.380
as models get scaled up
or more and more data

00:56:23.380 --> 00:56:26.290
is used to train them.

00:56:26.290 --> 00:56:28.390
Cool.

00:56:28.390 --> 00:56:34.940
So the last thing, this is no
longer specific hyperparameters,

00:56:34.940 --> 00:56:37.030
but two other things
that you might

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:40.000
need to do before you
set your model to run,

00:56:40.000 --> 00:56:43.040
which is dropout and other
kinds of regularization.

00:56:43.040 --> 00:56:45.670
And I think this one was
really interesting to me

00:56:45.670 --> 00:56:48.160
when I was originally doing
the research for putting

00:56:48.160 --> 00:56:49.840
this lecture together.

00:56:49.840 --> 00:56:52.230
If you think about
pre-training, pre-training

00:56:52.230 --> 00:56:53.980
is about the furthest
place that you might

00:56:53.980 --> 00:56:55.880
think of from regularization.

00:56:55.880 --> 00:56:59.820
Because pre-training,
you do usually one epoch.

00:56:59.820 --> 00:57:01.570
You can't even go
through all of your data

00:57:01.570 --> 00:57:02.903
because you have too much of it.

00:57:02.903 --> 00:57:05.130
So you're going to do
one epoch training,

00:57:05.130 --> 00:57:07.290
and you're almost
certainly not overfitting

00:57:07.290 --> 00:57:09.970
the data in that one
pass that you're doing.

00:57:09.970 --> 00:57:12.390
And so you might think, we
don't need regularization

00:57:12.390 --> 00:57:13.240
for pre-training.

00:57:13.240 --> 00:57:15.280
Let's just set your
optimizer loose.

00:57:15.280 --> 00:57:18.030
It's all about minimizing loss.

00:57:18.030 --> 00:57:21.180
And this is really
good arguments

00:57:21.180 --> 00:57:24.280
for why you shouldn't
need to regularize.

00:57:24.280 --> 00:57:27.540
But then, if you look
at what people do,

00:57:27.540 --> 00:57:31.110
the story is actually
kind of mixed.

00:57:31.110 --> 00:57:34.020
And this story actually
is maybe even more mixed

00:57:34.020 --> 00:57:36.520
than what has turned out to be.

00:57:36.520 --> 00:57:39.840
But early days, people
did a lot of dropout.

00:57:39.840 --> 00:57:42.300
And then there's a
lot of weight decay

00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:44.910
that also seems to be happening.

00:57:44.910 --> 00:57:47.220
And these days, I think,
a lot of the people

00:57:47.220 --> 00:57:49.560
have stopped publishing
details on precisely they're

00:57:49.560 --> 00:57:50.980
training hyperparameters.

00:57:50.980 --> 00:57:54.040
But dropout has
gone out of fashion.

00:57:54.040 --> 00:57:57.270
But weight decay has really been
something that a lot of people

00:57:57.270 --> 00:57:59.160
continue to do.

00:57:59.160 --> 00:58:00.430
And why is that?

00:58:00.430 --> 00:58:03.015
That's a really odd
thing to be doing.

00:58:03.015 --> 00:58:04.640
So I'll give you a
moment to just think

00:58:04.640 --> 00:58:06.170
about this state of affairs.

00:58:06.170 --> 00:58:09.470
If you're training a
really large neural network

00:58:09.470 --> 00:58:12.870
for one pass on SGD, on
vast amounts of data,

00:58:12.870 --> 00:58:16.230
why would You use weight
decay when you're doing that?

00:58:16.230 --> 00:58:18.593
So maybe some of the answer.

00:58:18.593 --> 00:58:20.510
But I think that's a
kind of interesting thing

00:58:20.510 --> 00:58:21.135
to think about.

00:58:21.135 --> 00:58:27.030
It's very intuition
violating, at least for me.

00:58:27.030 --> 00:58:33.650
So the reason is because it's
not to control overfitting.

00:58:33.650 --> 00:58:36.510
In the sense that if you
look at weight decay,

00:58:36.510 --> 00:58:38.150
different amounts
of weight decay

00:58:38.150 --> 00:58:41.930
don't really seem to change
the ratio of training loss

00:58:41.930 --> 00:58:43.110
to validation loss.

00:58:43.110 --> 00:58:45.440
So you can train with different
amounts of weight decay

00:58:45.440 --> 00:58:48.380
if you train for long enough or
you control your hyperparameters

00:58:48.380 --> 00:58:50.900
appropriately, you'll end
up with the same train

00:58:50.900 --> 00:58:52.140
to val loss gap.

00:58:52.140 --> 00:58:54.620
So overfitting, nothing's
happening here, even

00:58:54.620 --> 00:58:55.890
with zero weight decay.

00:58:55.890 --> 00:58:58.910
But what is interesting
is that the weight decay

00:58:58.910 --> 00:59:03.130
seems to be interacting
somewhat in a strange way

00:59:03.130 --> 00:59:06.820
with the learning rate
schedules of the optimizers.

00:59:06.820 --> 00:59:09.550
And so what's happening
is that if you

00:59:09.550 --> 00:59:13.520
look at a constant
learning rate,

00:59:13.520 --> 00:59:15.920
so this is a model trained
on constant learning rate.

00:59:15.920 --> 00:59:19.850
And then you suddenly decrease
the learning rate to near zero.

00:59:19.850 --> 00:59:23.440
So you see this drop off as
you decrease the learning rate.

00:59:23.440 --> 00:59:27.250
And then let's look at
different kinds of weight decay

00:59:27.250 --> 00:59:28.250
that you could do.

00:59:28.250 --> 00:59:30.250
And what happens is
with weight decay,

00:59:30.250 --> 00:59:33.265
the model is not training very
well at this high learning rate.

00:59:33.265 --> 00:59:35.140
And then when you decrease
the learning rate,

00:59:35.140 --> 00:59:37.310
it'll very rapidly drop off.

00:59:37.310 --> 00:59:40.750
And when you look at cosine
learning rate decay, what

00:59:40.750 --> 00:59:44.200
happens is that the models
with high weight decay

00:59:44.200 --> 00:59:45.647
start out very slow.

00:59:45.647 --> 00:59:47.980
But then as they cool down,
that is, their learning rate

00:59:47.980 --> 00:59:51.460
decreases, they very
rapidly optimize.

00:59:51.460 --> 00:59:55.930
And so there's some very complex
interaction happening here

00:59:55.930 --> 00:59:59.130
between the optimizer
and the weight decay

00:59:59.130 --> 01:00:01.680
and some of implicit
acceleration

01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:04.320
that happens near the tail
end of training that ends up

01:00:04.320 --> 01:00:06.220
giving you better models.

01:00:06.220 --> 01:00:07.710
And so the answer
to the question

01:00:07.710 --> 01:00:10.218
I posed you is you
don't weight decay

01:00:10.218 --> 01:00:12.510
because you want to regularize
the model, which is kind

01:00:12.510 --> 01:00:13.810
of what it was designed for.

01:00:13.810 --> 01:00:17.220
Your weight decaying in order
to get actually better training

01:00:17.220 --> 01:00:18.070
losses.

01:00:18.070 --> 01:00:21.150
And you end up doing that
because of the various learning

01:00:21.150 --> 01:00:23.910
dynamics at the tail end of
training, as you decrease

01:00:23.910 --> 01:00:25.120
your learning rates to 0.

01:00:25.120 --> 01:00:28.800
It's a very interesting
and complex.

01:00:28.800 --> 01:00:31.770
And in some ways,
troubling thing

01:00:31.770 --> 01:00:33.670
to be doing with
language models.

01:00:33.670 --> 01:00:37.748
But now, you see why, if you
look at a lot of the reports,

01:00:37.748 --> 01:00:39.040
you'll see we use weight decay.

01:00:39.040 --> 01:00:42.115
This is why that
ends up happening.

01:00:42.115 --> 01:00:42.615
Cool.

01:00:45.790 --> 01:00:50.430
So putting all that together, so
there are certain things that I

01:00:50.430 --> 01:00:52.300
think are just no-brainer.

01:00:52.300 --> 01:00:55.000
So if you're picking various
hyperparameters for your model.

01:00:55.000 --> 01:00:57.590
You don't really need to
think too deeply about them

01:00:57.590 --> 01:00:59.270
in the sense that
they've been validated

01:00:59.270 --> 01:01:00.853
and basically everyone
else does them.

01:01:00.853 --> 01:01:04.250
So this is things like
the hidden size of MLP,

01:01:04.250 --> 01:01:07.740
the head dimensions of
your multi-head attention,

01:01:07.740 --> 01:01:11.480
your aspect ratio, and your
choice of regularization

01:01:11.480 --> 01:01:12.480
through weight decay.

01:01:12.480 --> 01:01:14.760
All of those,
there's fairly good,

01:01:14.760 --> 01:01:17.000
I think, consensus
evidence of how

01:01:17.000 --> 01:01:18.950
to pick most of these
hyperparameters.

01:01:18.950 --> 01:01:21.683
And those defaults roughly
give you the kinds of things

01:01:21.683 --> 01:01:23.100
that we suggest
in the assignment.

01:01:23.100 --> 01:01:25.308
So you can follow along,
and they'll roughly give you

01:01:25.308 --> 01:01:27.650
something similar to this.

01:01:27.650 --> 01:01:33.830
So any questions about
the hyperparameter piece?

01:01:33.830 --> 01:01:34.920
Yes.

01:01:34.920 --> 01:01:38.000
Is there a reason why
dropouts gone out of fashion?

01:01:38.000 --> 01:01:39.620
That's a good question.

01:01:39.620 --> 01:01:40.970
I don't think I've seen--

01:01:40.970 --> 01:01:44.420
the question was, why did
dropout go out of fashion?

01:01:44.420 --> 01:01:47.150
I haven't quite seen
a deep analysis of why

01:01:47.150 --> 01:01:49.200
dropout is or isn't helpful.

01:01:49.200 --> 01:01:51.860
I haven't seen any result that,
for example, shows that it

01:01:51.860 --> 01:01:53.120
helps for training loss.

01:01:53.120 --> 01:01:57.250
And both this paper argues
and logic would dictate,

01:01:57.250 --> 01:02:00.340
there's not really a
training overfitting issue

01:02:00.340 --> 01:02:02.080
with these models
that can't even

01:02:02.080 --> 01:02:05.000
do one epoch over
their training data.

01:02:05.000 --> 01:02:05.500
Yes.

01:02:08.380 --> 01:02:10.690
Multilingual
vocabularies actually

01:02:10.690 --> 01:02:14.920
contribute to improve
performance in one language.

01:02:14.920 --> 01:02:17.330
So, [INAUDIBLE].

01:02:17.330 --> 01:02:19.840
So the question was, do
multilingual vocabularies

01:02:19.840 --> 01:02:22.400
contribute to improving
performance in one language?

01:02:22.400 --> 01:02:23.858
When you say one
language, you mean

01:02:23.858 --> 01:02:27.070
do multilingual or larger
vocabularies help performance

01:02:27.070 --> 01:02:27.800
in English?

01:02:27.800 --> 01:02:28.980
Is that the right question?

01:02:28.980 --> 01:02:29.830
Yeah.

01:02:29.830 --> 01:02:34.310
So I think in your high resource
language, the impact is less.

01:02:34.310 --> 01:02:38.020
So if you're only thinking
about English language language

01:02:38.020 --> 01:02:41.360
modeling, you can get away
with smaller vocabularies.

01:02:41.360 --> 01:02:43.660
This much is kind of true.

01:02:43.660 --> 01:02:47.140
But the place where larger
vocabularies is really helpful

01:02:47.140 --> 01:02:48.740
is when you're
starting to get at,

01:02:48.740 --> 01:02:50.630
I wouldn't say the tail
of your distribution,

01:02:50.630 --> 01:02:53.650
but when you get to languages
that are more minority.

01:02:53.650 --> 01:02:55.500
And one great example
of this, if you

01:02:55.500 --> 01:02:57.990
look at any of the
Cohere announcements

01:02:57.990 --> 01:03:00.300
about their models
or their tokenizers,

01:03:00.300 --> 01:03:02.790
they basically always argue
that because of the way

01:03:02.790 --> 01:03:04.770
they have larger
vocabularies and the way

01:03:04.770 --> 01:03:08.550
they train their tokenizer,
non-English and low resource

01:03:08.550 --> 01:03:11.890
languages, they are packed
into much fewer tokens.

01:03:11.890 --> 01:03:17.190
And so people using
those pay much lower cost

01:03:17.190 --> 01:03:21.407
at inference time, which
is a great benefit.

01:03:21.407 --> 01:03:21.990
Yes, question?

01:03:21.990 --> 01:03:25.170
[INAUDIBLE] if
weight decay doesn't

01:03:25.170 --> 01:03:27.960
have a significant impact
on the vali loss, why do we

01:03:27.960 --> 01:03:30.960
care about the training dynamics
or the favorable [INAUDIBLE]

01:03:30.960 --> 01:03:31.973
dynamics?

01:03:31.973 --> 01:03:33.390
So the question
was, if it doesn't

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:34.973
have an impact on
vali loss, why do we

01:03:34.973 --> 01:03:36.630
care about training dynamics?

01:03:36.630 --> 01:03:41.500
The goal is still, I want
to get good training loss.

01:03:41.500 --> 01:03:43.000
This is the game
that we're playing.

01:03:43.000 --> 01:03:44.820
And the surprising
thing about weight decay

01:03:44.820 --> 01:03:47.317
is that somehow, it gets
us better training losses.

01:03:47.317 --> 01:03:48.900
I think the intuitive
thing that makes

01:03:48.900 --> 01:03:50.720
sense is, you do weight decay.

01:03:50.720 --> 01:03:52.103
It gives you better vali losses.

01:03:52.103 --> 01:03:53.270
But that's not what happens.

01:03:53.270 --> 01:03:55.990
What it's getting you is better
training losses, which are also

01:03:55.990 --> 01:03:57.100
the same as vali losses.

01:04:00.844 --> 01:04:02.230
Yes.

01:04:02.230 --> 01:04:05.740
Are there differences in the
architecture hyperparameter

01:04:05.740 --> 01:04:07.930
choices people make
as they move towards

01:04:07.930 --> 01:04:10.180
multimodal
architectures, if they're

01:04:10.180 --> 01:04:12.460
doing images as well as text?

01:04:12.460 --> 01:04:14.990
So the question was
about multimodal models.

01:04:14.990 --> 01:04:16.370
That is a great question.

01:04:16.370 --> 01:04:20.470
My survey of multimodal
models is very incomplete.

01:04:20.470 --> 01:04:24.100
What I can say is, a lot of the
academic and open work that I've

01:04:24.100 --> 01:04:26.710
seen, they do what
you might call

01:04:26.710 --> 01:04:29.920
like shallow or later
fusion or earlier

01:04:29.920 --> 01:04:31.760
fusion of the modalities.

01:04:31.760 --> 01:04:34.480
And the way that works is
you bolt the vision modality

01:04:34.480 --> 01:04:35.985
onto an existing language model.

01:04:35.985 --> 01:04:38.110
In those cases, the
hyperparameter and architecture

01:04:38.110 --> 01:04:39.910
choices are fixed.

01:04:39.910 --> 01:04:41.290
One thing I will
note, and I will

01:04:41.290 --> 01:04:43.960
talk about this in
just a few slides,

01:04:43.960 --> 01:04:46.420
is that the multimodal
models pioneered

01:04:46.420 --> 01:04:49.740
some pretty interesting
techniques in stabilizing

01:04:49.740 --> 01:04:50.860
language model training.

01:04:50.860 --> 01:04:52.330
And that's been a
really big theme,

01:04:52.330 --> 01:04:53.955
and I'll talk a little
bit about those.

01:04:53.955 --> 01:04:57.690
So what is different is often
when you bolt on this new vision

01:04:57.690 --> 01:05:00.803
piece and you retrain with that,
that's a big shock to the model.

01:05:00.803 --> 01:05:02.220
And so you have
to think carefully

01:05:02.220 --> 01:05:04.150
about how to stabilize
that training process.

01:05:04.150 --> 01:05:06.108
And those innovations
have actually seeped back

01:05:06.108 --> 01:05:08.225
into pure text language
model training.

01:05:11.613 --> 01:05:12.113
Cool.

01:05:15.510 --> 01:05:19.200
So I went back
through and I looked

01:05:19.200 --> 01:05:20.530
through all these new papers.

01:05:20.530 --> 01:05:22.155
And as I was trying
to think about, OK,

01:05:22.155 --> 01:05:23.880
what's been new
in the last year?

01:05:23.880 --> 01:05:27.040
And what new architecture and
related things have happened?

01:05:27.040 --> 01:05:30.220
Actually, the core architecture
hasn't changed much.

01:05:30.220 --> 01:05:32.040
But I think the one
thing that stood out

01:05:32.040 --> 01:05:35.640
as being very emphasized
in a lot of the releases

01:05:35.640 --> 01:05:38.920
has been what I would
call stability tricks.

01:05:38.920 --> 01:05:40.830
And so these are
things where you

01:05:40.830 --> 01:05:44.790
would like to train your model
in much more stable ways.

01:05:44.790 --> 01:05:46.830
And as you make bigger
and bigger models,

01:05:46.830 --> 01:05:48.660
you train for longer and longer.

01:05:48.660 --> 01:05:51.270
These kinds of issues start
to appear more and more.

01:05:51.270 --> 01:05:55.400
So I've taken this
from the OLMo 2 paper.

01:05:55.400 --> 01:05:59.900
And actually, that paper is a
great set of academic results

01:05:59.900 --> 01:06:02.660
on LLM training stability.

01:06:02.660 --> 01:06:06.510
And one thing they start
with is this figure.

01:06:06.510 --> 01:06:08.520
And you look at this
blue curve over here.

01:06:08.520 --> 01:06:12.240
And you look at this, L2
norm of the gradient graph.

01:06:12.240 --> 01:06:14.730
And this is terrifying
graph to look at.

01:06:14.730 --> 01:06:18.630
Your loss curve kind of
seems to be behaving OK.

01:06:18.630 --> 01:06:21.270
But you've got some bad
spikes every now and then.

01:06:21.270 --> 01:06:23.160
And you open up
your gradient norm,

01:06:23.160 --> 01:06:24.830
and it's this
horrible plot where

01:06:24.830 --> 01:06:27.500
you've got spikes everywhere,
where your norms are completely

01:06:27.500 --> 01:06:28.940
blowing up.

01:06:28.940 --> 01:06:31.470
And if you're training
models like this,

01:06:31.470 --> 01:06:33.500
you're going to have a
really tough time getting

01:06:33.500 --> 01:06:35.010
it to converge reasonably.

01:06:35.010 --> 01:06:37.130
At some point,
it's going to hit--

01:06:37.130 --> 01:06:39.680
gradient norm explodes,
and you can't do anything.

01:06:39.680 --> 01:06:43.040
And your training is done so
you can't train any further.

01:06:43.040 --> 01:06:45.760
And so there's been a lot
of emphasis basically trying

01:06:45.760 --> 01:06:48.130
to turn this blue curve
into something that looks

01:06:48.130 --> 01:06:49.877
a lot like the orange curve.

01:06:49.877 --> 01:06:52.210
And of course, this loss is
higher, but ignore that fact

01:06:52.210 --> 01:06:53.627
because I think
they just switched

01:06:53.627 --> 01:06:55.570
data sets in between
these two training runs.

01:06:55.570 --> 01:06:59.943
But this orange curve has nice
low gradient norms throughout.

01:06:59.943 --> 01:07:01.360
And that's really
the kind of plot

01:07:01.360 --> 01:07:03.940
that you would much rather see.

01:07:03.940 --> 01:07:07.450
And so you might ask, where
do stability issues arise

01:07:07.450 --> 01:07:08.420
in transformers?

01:07:08.420 --> 01:07:11.590
And of course, they can
arise basically everywhere.

01:07:11.590 --> 01:07:13.960
But if you look at the
kind of interventions

01:07:13.960 --> 01:07:16.540
that people are
making, there's really

01:07:16.540 --> 01:07:20.180
one place that really stands out
as the kind of problem child.

01:07:20.180 --> 01:07:22.330
And that's the softmaxes.

01:07:22.330 --> 01:07:24.370
And it can be a problem
because you're going

01:07:24.370 --> 01:07:25.700
to be taking exponentials.

01:07:25.700 --> 01:07:29.080
And those can be
numerically badly behaved.

01:07:29.080 --> 01:07:30.837
You're also dividing
two numbers.

01:07:30.837 --> 01:07:32.420
And so you might
have a division by 0.

01:07:32.420 --> 01:07:35.410
So for many different
reasons, this softmax piece

01:07:35.410 --> 01:07:40.330
is a part that you might
have lots of issues with.

01:07:40.330 --> 01:07:43.180
And so, actually, one more
thing I want to talk about.

01:07:43.180 --> 01:07:46.090
So where are the softmaxes
in a transformer?

01:07:46.090 --> 01:07:47.710
Well, there's one
at the very end.

01:07:47.710 --> 01:07:50.260
So you've got to be careful
about that output softmax.

01:07:50.260 --> 01:07:53.740
And also, there's softmaxes
in your self-attention.

01:07:53.740 --> 01:07:55.513
So there's two softmaxes
that we're going

01:07:55.513 --> 01:07:56.680
to think a little bit about.

01:07:56.680 --> 01:07:59.070
And for each one,
I'm going to mention

01:07:59.070 --> 01:08:01.950
stability intervention
that has generally

01:08:01.950 --> 01:08:05.290
seemed to be effective.

01:08:05.290 --> 01:08:08.520
So the first one is
called the z-loss.

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:11.440
And it might desire to
cite a paper that's older.

01:08:11.440 --> 01:08:14.850
I've gone back to
Devlin in 2014,

01:08:14.850 --> 01:08:18.120
where in a machine
translation paper,

01:08:18.120 --> 01:08:22.800
their goal was to try to make
sure that this normalizer was

01:08:22.800 --> 01:08:23.710
near 1.

01:08:23.710 --> 01:08:27.779
So if you look at p of x, that's
the output softmax over here.

01:08:27.779 --> 01:08:29.649
The output softmax is two terms.

01:08:29.649 --> 01:08:31.529
You exponentiate your
logits, and then you

01:08:31.529 --> 01:08:33.250
divide by the normalizer z.

01:08:33.250 --> 01:08:37.540
And the z is just summing up
the values across all the vocab.

01:08:37.540 --> 01:08:39.880
And so if you want
this z of x, you

01:08:39.880 --> 01:08:43.620
want to train the network to
have a z of x close to one.

01:08:43.620 --> 01:08:45.840
Well, then you can
rewrite your loss,

01:08:45.840 --> 01:08:47.779
and you can add a
little second term here

01:08:47.779 --> 01:08:52.109
to try to force log of z
of xi to be close to 0.

01:08:52.109 --> 01:08:54.109
So you're going to end
up with an auxiliary loss

01:08:54.109 --> 01:08:57.479
term that's alpha log
squared z of xi right.

01:08:57.479 --> 01:09:00.229
You can see that derivation
on the right here.

01:09:00.229 --> 01:09:04.670
And this is in some sense what
people often call the z-loss.

01:09:04.670 --> 01:09:06.859
I think Jacob Devlin
and others did

01:09:06.859 --> 01:09:09.290
this for machine translation
for totally different reasons

01:09:09.290 --> 01:09:11.359
than what it's used for today.

01:09:11.359 --> 01:09:14.689
But this was, I think, the first
instance of this in language

01:09:14.689 --> 01:09:18.240
modeling land was PaLM, who
used this, as they called it,

01:09:18.240 --> 01:09:21.470
auxiliary loss of z-loss
10 to the negative 4 log

01:09:21.470 --> 01:09:24.680
squared z to basically
encourage the softmax normalizer

01:09:24.680 --> 01:09:26.359
to behave nicely.

01:09:26.359 --> 01:09:29.960
And you can reason through the
behavior of this regularizer.

01:09:29.960 --> 01:09:34.590
If it succeeds and it forces
log of z of x to always be 0,

01:09:34.590 --> 01:09:37.990
then the log and the
exponential cancels,

01:09:37.990 --> 01:09:40.310
and you've basically
just got U of r of x.

01:09:40.310 --> 01:09:41.560
And that's a good place to be.

01:09:41.560 --> 01:09:43.790
That's a nice numerically
stable operation.

01:09:43.790 --> 01:09:46.760
So all of these problematic
operations go away.

01:09:46.760 --> 01:09:48.430
And so you can
think of the softmax

01:09:48.430 --> 01:09:52.660
as being well-behaved
when z of x is close to 1.

01:09:52.660 --> 01:09:55.378
Or log of z is close to 0.

01:09:55.378 --> 01:09:58.960
And PaLM, in some sense,
is very much a pioneer.

01:09:58.960 --> 01:10:02.530
Because they did this z
loss trick, and many others

01:10:02.530 --> 01:10:04.930
didn't really do it for a long
time, or at least the ones

01:10:04.930 --> 01:10:06.508
that had open papers.

01:10:06.508 --> 01:10:08.050
But then there was
a kind of sequence

01:10:08.050 --> 01:10:09.730
of papers that have done this.

01:10:09.730 --> 01:10:12.010
Baichuan 2 is
actually the earliest

01:10:12.010 --> 01:10:13.190
follow up that I know of.

01:10:13.190 --> 01:10:16.300
And then DCLM and OLMo
2 and now several others

01:10:16.300 --> 01:10:17.950
have basically
picked up on z-loss.

01:10:17.950 --> 01:10:21.310
So a very nice, convenient
intervention for improving

01:10:21.310 --> 01:10:23.500
stability.

01:10:23.500 --> 01:10:26.600
And then, the other
trick that we see,

01:10:26.600 --> 01:10:31.250
so that was how to stabilize
the output softmax.

01:10:31.250 --> 01:10:33.620
But we've got another softmax
we've got to deal with.

01:10:33.620 --> 01:10:35.680
The other softmax
we have to deal with

01:10:35.680 --> 01:10:38.440
is in the attention operation.

01:10:38.440 --> 01:10:41.740
And so this is from
an NVIDIA paper.

01:10:41.740 --> 01:10:44.050
I forgot to put the
citation marker.

01:10:44.050 --> 01:10:48.340
But here, this is a block
diagram of how attention works.

01:10:48.340 --> 01:10:50.810
You got your layer
norm at the beginning.

01:10:50.810 --> 01:10:52.510
You got your QKVs.

01:10:52.510 --> 01:10:54.140
Ignore this for the moment.

01:10:54.140 --> 01:10:56.870
You might multiply
your Qs and your Ks.

01:10:56.870 --> 01:10:58.040
You'll softmax it.

01:10:58.040 --> 01:11:00.640
You'll multiply the V, and
then you'll project it.

01:11:00.640 --> 01:11:02.890
And then that's going to
give you your fully connected

01:11:02.890 --> 01:11:03.560
and your output.

01:11:03.560 --> 01:11:07.240
So if you ignore this
little piece over here,

01:11:07.240 --> 01:11:11.140
this looks just like your normal
multi-head attention operation.

01:11:11.140 --> 01:11:14.500
So what's the difference here?

01:11:14.500 --> 01:11:18.460
So several folks came up with
this idea or this approach

01:11:18.460 --> 01:11:22.240
called the QK norm, where you
take the queries and the keys,

01:11:22.240 --> 01:11:24.640
and you pass them
through a LayerNorm layer

01:11:24.640 --> 01:11:27.490
before you take their inner
product for the softmax

01:11:27.490 --> 01:11:28.750
operation.

01:11:28.750 --> 01:11:30.670
And this is a very
different kind

01:11:30.670 --> 01:11:35.000
of approach to controlling
the behavior of the softmax.

01:11:35.000 --> 01:11:37.260
Here, you're not controlling
the normalizer z.

01:11:37.260 --> 01:11:39.860
Instead, you're controlling
the inputs to the softmax

01:11:39.860 --> 01:11:42.030
to be bounded in size.

01:11:42.030 --> 01:11:45.440
And that's going to naturally
control the bad behaviors

01:11:45.440 --> 01:11:47.210
of the softmax.

01:11:47.210 --> 01:11:49.040
And as I said before,
this is originally

01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:53.510
an innovation from the vision
and multimodal model community.

01:11:53.510 --> 01:11:56.910
Dehgani in 2023, this
was a paper on training,

01:11:56.910 --> 01:11:59.180
very large vision transformers.

01:11:59.180 --> 01:12:03.620
And then Chameleon and
Idefcs from Hugging

01:12:03.620 --> 01:12:07.550
Face use these tricks for their
multi-modal training components.

01:12:07.550 --> 01:12:10.730
And then it got picked
up by several others

01:12:10.730 --> 01:12:14.390
like Gemma 2, DCLM,
OLMo 2, all basically

01:12:14.390 --> 01:12:17.990
uses this kind of
techniques in order

01:12:17.990 --> 01:12:21.290
to stabilize their training.

01:12:21.290 --> 01:12:25.350
And I think I'm allowed to
add one joke per lecture.

01:12:25.350 --> 01:12:27.470
And so this is the one
I'm going to go with here.

01:12:27.470 --> 01:12:30.260
I think one of the things
that really has stood out

01:12:30.260 --> 01:12:31.870
in terms of stability
interventions

01:12:31.870 --> 01:12:35.480
has been just how strikingly
effective LayerNorms are.

01:12:35.480 --> 01:12:39.970
So we've seen going from
LayerNorms just in the pre

01:12:39.970 --> 01:12:43.240
part of the block to
both the beginning

01:12:43.240 --> 01:12:45.680
and the end of the non
residual component.

01:12:45.680 --> 01:12:49.510
And now, we've also thrown it
into the Q and the K component.

01:12:49.510 --> 01:12:51.710
At least in terms of
improving stability,

01:12:51.710 --> 01:12:55.300
LayerNorms have been shockingly
effective without affecting

01:12:55.300 --> 01:12:57.610
performance too much.

01:12:57.610 --> 01:13:00.100
The last trick that
I'll note, I think

01:13:00.100 --> 01:13:04.750
this one has been not
quite as frequently used,

01:13:04.750 --> 01:13:10.610
which is to soft-cap the logits
that go into the softmax.

01:13:10.610 --> 01:13:13.600
So the other approach that you
can take, so QK norm is in some

01:13:13.600 --> 01:13:15.910
sense a very heavy
handed intervention

01:13:15.910 --> 01:13:19.000
because we're going to operate
over the entire vector.

01:13:19.000 --> 01:13:20.680
But one thing you
could do is after you

01:13:20.680 --> 01:13:23.000
take the inner products
for self-attention,

01:13:23.000 --> 01:13:26.420
you could pass them through
a soft maximum operation.

01:13:26.420 --> 01:13:29.210
So you can pass them through
this equation over here.

01:13:29.210 --> 01:13:31.130
So you have your
logits as your input.

01:13:31.130 --> 01:13:33.060
Divide it by the soft cap.

01:13:33.060 --> 01:13:34.650
Multiply by the soft cap.

01:13:34.650 --> 01:13:35.520
What does that do?

01:13:35.520 --> 01:13:39.090
Well, if your logits start
exceeding the soft cap by a lot,

01:13:39.090 --> 01:13:41.310
the tanh is going to
clip them off to one.

01:13:41.310 --> 01:13:43.430
And so you're going to
have a maximum value

01:13:43.430 --> 01:13:45.630
of soft cap over here.

01:13:45.630 --> 01:13:48.830
So this is going to control,
in some sense, soft clipping

01:13:48.830 --> 01:13:54.290
of the logits and Gemma 2, and
I think OLMo 2 also do this.

01:13:54.290 --> 01:13:58.680
It hasn't been I think
quite as popular otherwise.

01:13:58.680 --> 01:14:02.690
And I think the other evidence
against this, the NVIDIA

01:14:02.690 --> 01:14:04.880
folks that I mentioned
earlier, did actually

01:14:04.880 --> 01:14:08.670
quite a few different stability
improving interventions.

01:14:08.670 --> 01:14:11.850
And what they find is you have
your baseline model over here.

01:14:11.850 --> 01:14:15.450
This is the perplexity of
the baseline model, 11.19.

01:14:15.450 --> 01:14:17.160
Soft capping makes it worse.

01:14:17.160 --> 01:14:19.040
QK norm actually makes
it better because you

01:14:19.040 --> 01:14:20.630
can use more aggressive
learning rates

01:14:20.630 --> 01:14:22.465
and push the optimizer further.

01:14:25.400 --> 01:14:26.370
Cool.

01:14:26.370 --> 01:14:30.730
So that's the end of the
stability improving intervention

01:14:30.730 --> 01:14:31.450
stuff.

01:14:31.450 --> 01:14:33.500
Does anyone have any questions?

01:14:33.500 --> 01:14:36.160
I think that's been kind
of the new development

01:14:36.160 --> 01:14:37.040
over the last year.

01:14:37.040 --> 01:14:37.550
Yes.

01:14:37.550 --> 01:14:40.012
So for the QKV
norm, I understand

01:14:40.012 --> 01:14:41.470
that during training,
you will have

01:14:41.470 --> 01:14:42.910
the LayerNorm being applied.

01:14:42.910 --> 01:14:46.270
At inference time, it's the
layer norm still being kept?

01:14:46.270 --> 01:14:46.850
Yes.

01:14:46.850 --> 01:14:48.200
So the question was,
at inference time,

01:14:48.200 --> 01:14:49.400
do you still use the norm?

01:14:49.400 --> 01:14:50.480
And the answer is yes.

01:14:50.480 --> 01:14:53.470
Because the layer norm
has learned parameters.

01:14:53.470 --> 01:14:55.540
Like the whole
action of the layer

01:14:55.540 --> 01:14:58.400
norm is it takes an activation,
normalizes it to a unit,

01:14:58.400 --> 01:14:59.900
and then scales
them to some size.

01:14:59.900 --> 01:15:02.240
If you take that out, that's
a huge change to the model.

01:15:02.240 --> 01:15:03.740
It will have no
idea what to do with

01:15:03.740 --> 01:15:05.266
those unnormalized activations.

01:15:09.333 --> 01:15:09.833
Cool.

01:15:12.440 --> 01:15:16.480
So I have this last
bit, last few slides

01:15:16.480 --> 01:15:17.960
that I want to end with.

01:15:17.960 --> 01:15:20.410
If we go over
then, we can always

01:15:20.410 --> 01:15:22.488
push this into the MOE lecture.

01:15:22.488 --> 01:15:25.030
But I think we also have a lot
of content next time because I

01:15:25.030 --> 01:15:27.840
have to cover deep seq V3.

01:15:27.840 --> 01:15:29.760
So the last thing
I want to cover

01:15:29.760 --> 01:15:32.490
is variations on
the attention heads.

01:15:32.490 --> 01:15:34.260
So attention heads,
I think, haven't

01:15:34.260 --> 01:15:37.980
had as much work done to them.

01:15:37.980 --> 01:15:40.770
But there have been a few,
I think, important changes

01:15:40.770 --> 01:15:42.270
that you need to
know about in order

01:15:42.270 --> 01:15:44.950
to understand the models
that are being trained.

01:15:44.950 --> 01:15:46.480
So the one thing
I'll talk about--

01:15:46.480 --> 01:15:49.500
the first thing I'll talk
about is GQA and MQA.

01:15:49.500 --> 01:15:52.512
And these aren't really
critical to the training time

01:15:52.512 --> 01:15:53.970
behavior of the
models, but they're

01:15:53.970 --> 01:15:57.150
very important in understanding
the inference costs

01:15:57.150 --> 01:15:58.840
and inference behavior
of the models.

01:15:58.840 --> 01:16:01.150
And because this is an
important architecture change,

01:16:01.150 --> 01:16:03.210
I'll mention them
here in addition to

01:16:03.210 --> 01:16:05.070
probably being mentioned
by Percy in some

01:16:05.070 --> 01:16:06.840
of the inference lectures.

01:16:06.840 --> 01:16:09.480
The other thing that's a
new development I'll mention

01:16:09.480 --> 01:16:12.930
is how the most recent models,
like LLaMA 4, if you've

01:16:12.930 --> 01:16:16.300
heard of it, supports supposedly
10 million tokens of context.

01:16:16.300 --> 01:16:17.260
How does it do that?

01:16:17.260 --> 01:16:20.340
Well, it does so by messing
with the attention pattern

01:16:20.340 --> 01:16:22.050
in very structured ways.

01:16:22.050 --> 01:16:26.570
So I'll talk about that as well.

01:16:26.570 --> 01:16:31.650
So GQA and MQA, if you looked
at some of the larger models,

01:16:31.650 --> 01:16:33.570
like the big LLaMA
models or others,

01:16:33.570 --> 01:16:37.250
you'll have heard or seen
this term GQA or MQA.

01:16:37.250 --> 01:16:40.260
And I'll talk through
what that means.

01:16:40.260 --> 01:16:43.310
So to set the stage, let's
think about the compute

01:16:43.310 --> 01:16:45.390
that you need to do attention.

01:16:45.390 --> 01:16:48.560
So this is, once again,
224N slides here.

01:16:48.560 --> 01:16:52.380
You're going to take your
XQ, your query, and your XK.

01:16:52.380 --> 01:16:55.370
And then you're going to form
your big quadratic attention

01:16:55.370 --> 01:16:56.130
matrix.

01:16:56.130 --> 01:16:59.070
And you can walk through each
of these matrix multiplies.

01:16:59.070 --> 01:17:01.400
And you can convince yourself
that the total number

01:17:01.400 --> 01:17:05.690
of arithmetic operations is
going to be b times n times

01:17:05.690 --> 01:17:06.450
d squared.

01:17:06.450 --> 01:17:09.710
So that's going to be b
is the batch dimension.

01:17:09.710 --> 01:17:11.760
n is the sequence length.

01:17:11.760 --> 01:17:17.120
And d squared is going to be
the hidden dimension squared.

01:17:17.120 --> 01:17:20.160
And you can ask about the
total memory accesses.

01:17:20.160 --> 01:17:22.310
And this is going to
be b times n times d.

01:17:22.310 --> 01:17:24.280
And this is going to be,
for example accessing

01:17:24.280 --> 01:17:25.910
just this matrix here.

01:17:25.910 --> 01:17:28.370
This XQ is going
to be that size.

01:17:28.370 --> 01:17:31.168
And then the softmax is going
to be b times h times n squared.

01:17:31.168 --> 01:17:32.710
And you can convince
yourself of that

01:17:32.710 --> 01:17:36.370
by just thinking about the size
of the softmax matrix, which

01:17:36.370 --> 01:17:38.320
is going to be batch
times number of heads

01:17:38.320 --> 01:17:40.630
times all of the
different softmax

01:17:40.630 --> 01:17:41.930
activations that you have.

01:17:41.930 --> 01:17:43.775
So that's n squared of them.

01:17:43.775 --> 01:17:45.400
And you've got a
projection, and you've

01:17:45.400 --> 01:17:48.700
got d squared projection
operations at the very end

01:17:48.700 --> 01:17:50.020
over here.

01:17:50.020 --> 01:17:54.490
And so we can take the ratio
of total memory accesses

01:17:54.490 --> 01:17:56.830
and arithmetic operations.

01:17:56.830 --> 01:17:58.210
And this is going
to be something

01:17:58.210 --> 01:18:01.010
that will be very important
in a couple of lectures,

01:18:01.010 --> 01:18:03.470
this idea called
arithmetic intensity.

01:18:03.470 --> 01:18:06.530
So we want our arithmetic
intensity to be high.

01:18:06.530 --> 01:18:08.080
What that means
is, we want to be

01:18:08.080 --> 01:18:12.440
doing a lot of compute for every
single memory access that we do.

01:18:12.440 --> 01:18:14.620
And this is going to be
because memory accesses are

01:18:14.620 --> 01:18:17.270
very expensive on a GPU,
relatively speaking.

01:18:17.270 --> 01:18:19.710
And compute is relatively cheap.

01:18:19.710 --> 01:18:23.720
And so in this batch computation
that I'm showing you here,

01:18:23.720 --> 01:18:26.220
the arithmetic intensity, if
you take the ratio of those two

01:18:26.220 --> 01:18:31.450
things, is going to be 1 over
k plus 1 over bn inverse.

01:18:31.450 --> 01:18:32.970
And so this is going
to mean that we

01:18:32.970 --> 01:18:35.473
can keep our GPUs running.

01:18:38.100 --> 01:18:40.500
Because if we have of
large number of heads

01:18:40.500 --> 01:18:44.040
and we have large batch size
and large sequence length,

01:18:44.040 --> 01:18:48.000
those are all going to
be good large numbers.

01:18:48.000 --> 01:18:51.000
Of course, this is what
happens at training time.

01:18:51.000 --> 01:18:52.900
So the issue is
that inference time,

01:18:52.900 --> 01:18:56.380
we do not have these big chunky
matrices to multiply together.

01:18:56.380 --> 01:18:58.110
And so that's going
to really change

01:18:58.110 --> 01:19:01.360
the nature of the behavior
of our algorithms.

01:19:01.360 --> 01:19:04.710
So when we're generating
text, remember that we

01:19:04.710 --> 01:19:06.040
have to generate a token.

01:19:06.040 --> 01:19:08.290
And then the transformer
has to read that token.

01:19:08.290 --> 01:19:09.880
And then it has to process it.

01:19:09.880 --> 01:19:12.203
And now, we can get the
next token distribution.

01:19:12.203 --> 01:19:14.370
And then we do the things
autoregressively one token

01:19:14.370 --> 01:19:14.920
at a time.

01:19:14.920 --> 01:19:18.370
And by doing this, we can't
parallelize this generation

01:19:18.370 --> 01:19:18.870
process.

01:19:18.870 --> 01:19:21.980
We need to go step by step
for every single new token.

01:19:21.980 --> 01:19:25.310
And when we do this, we're going
to need to incrementally compute

01:19:25.310 --> 01:19:29.150
attention, an idea that
people call the KV cache.

01:19:29.150 --> 01:19:30.980
And so what do you do?

01:19:30.980 --> 01:19:35.070
This is a lovely animation of a
KV cache that's been explained.

01:19:35.070 --> 01:19:39.530
So if you can look at this
figure, what you're doing

01:19:39.530 --> 01:19:42.800
is you've got a query token.

01:19:42.800 --> 01:19:45.810
A query token here is you've
generated a new token.

01:19:45.810 --> 01:19:46.980
You're conditioning on it.

01:19:46.980 --> 01:19:49.430
And now, you want to ask,
what information should I

01:19:49.430 --> 01:19:51.870
look up in the past,
past that query token?

01:19:51.870 --> 01:19:54.173
And your query tokens
are shifting from 1

01:19:54.173 --> 01:19:56.090
through n because you're
generating new tokens

01:19:56.090 --> 01:19:57.290
one at a time.

01:19:57.290 --> 01:20:00.690
You're building up this
key cache over here,

01:20:00.690 --> 01:20:04.590
where, basically, I'm building
up all of the past tokens keys.

01:20:04.590 --> 01:20:07.520
And the past tokens keys
don't change because they only

01:20:07.520 --> 01:20:09.120
depend on things in the past.

01:20:09.120 --> 01:20:12.590
And so, incrementally, as I
generate tokens, building up

01:20:12.590 --> 01:20:14.370
all of these past keys.

01:20:14.370 --> 01:20:18.490
And each time, I can compute
one new element of Q dot K.

01:20:18.490 --> 01:20:20.260
So the big attention
matrix is going

01:20:20.260 --> 01:20:22.850
to be this lower
triangular matrix.

01:20:22.850 --> 01:20:24.590
I'm computing one row at a time.

01:20:24.590 --> 01:20:27.190
And that row is exactly
what's necessary to generate

01:20:27.190 --> 01:20:28.220
the next token.

01:20:28.220 --> 01:20:31.995
So this KV cache idea, if
you've not seen this before,

01:20:31.995 --> 01:20:33.370
is this idea of
saying, I'm going

01:20:33.370 --> 01:20:37.310
to generate the K's and the
V's incrementally as I go,

01:20:37.310 --> 01:20:38.810
as I generate each token.

01:20:38.810 --> 01:20:42.700
And I'm only going to
compute QK that's absolutely

01:20:42.700 --> 01:20:45.820
necessary to do my operations.

01:20:45.820 --> 01:20:47.860
And so once again,
you can go through

01:20:47.860 --> 01:20:52.000
and do the various
arithmetic components

01:20:52.000 --> 01:20:54.650
of how many flops do we do?

01:20:54.650 --> 01:20:56.900
What's the total number
of memory accesses?

01:20:56.900 --> 01:20:59.200
And if you think
about the KV cache,

01:20:59.200 --> 01:21:02.200
I'm only multiplying the
absolute necessary keys

01:21:02.200 --> 01:21:02.900
and values.

01:21:02.900 --> 01:21:06.350
Since I'm saving all of the
intermediate computations,

01:21:06.350 --> 01:21:10.480
I'm not wasting any matrix
or vector vector multiplies.

01:21:10.480 --> 01:21:12.580
The total number of
arithmetic operations

01:21:12.580 --> 01:21:15.640
remains exactly the
same, bnd squared.

01:21:15.640 --> 01:21:19.360
But the memory access
patterns are now different.

01:21:19.360 --> 01:21:20.260
Why is that?

01:21:20.260 --> 01:21:23.170
Because when I do
this KV caching thing,

01:21:23.170 --> 01:21:26.100
I'm going to have to move
various kinds of parameters

01:21:26.100 --> 01:21:27.790
in and out of memory repeatedly.

01:21:27.790 --> 01:21:31.510
Whenever I multiply
with a key matrix,

01:21:31.510 --> 01:21:33.480
I'm going to have to
put that into memory,

01:21:33.480 --> 01:21:36.065
and then multiply by K. And
then I need to put that away,

01:21:36.065 --> 01:21:37.690
and I need to compute
some activations.

01:21:37.690 --> 01:21:40.418
And so I'm repeatedly loading
in different matrices.

01:21:40.418 --> 01:21:42.960
And that's going to give me a
much higher total memory access

01:21:42.960 --> 01:21:46.020
of b squared d plus nd squared.

01:21:46.020 --> 01:21:49.500
And so when you take this ratio,
now, the arithmetic intensity

01:21:49.500 --> 01:21:50.770
is not so good.

01:21:50.770 --> 01:21:54.840
You're going to get n over
d plus 1 over b inverse.

01:21:54.840 --> 01:21:57.340
And so if we reason
through this--

01:21:57.340 --> 01:22:00.300
so if I want arithmetic
intensity to be high,

01:22:00.300 --> 01:22:02.470
I want this thing
inside to be very small.

01:22:02.470 --> 01:22:04.420
So I need really large batches.

01:22:04.420 --> 01:22:07.337
And I need n over d to be small.

01:22:07.337 --> 01:22:08.170
What does that mean?

01:22:08.170 --> 01:22:11.340
I need really short sequence
lengths or really big model

01:22:11.340 --> 01:22:12.390
dimensions.

01:22:12.390 --> 01:22:15.680
And this n over d is
really unfavorable

01:22:15.680 --> 01:22:17.300
because I don't
want a bigger model,

01:22:17.300 --> 01:22:19.350
and I don't want a
shorter sequence length.

01:22:19.350 --> 01:22:21.740
And so this is the
core, in some sense,

01:22:21.740 --> 01:22:24.360
inference cost trade
off that people face.

01:22:24.360 --> 01:22:27.530
You have this very bad
memory access pattern

01:22:27.530 --> 01:22:29.760
where you have this
one term, n over d,

01:22:29.760 --> 01:22:32.060
that's really
killing you in terms

01:22:32.060 --> 01:22:35.060
of the throughput
of your system.

01:22:35.060 --> 01:22:38.750
And so this motivates
this thing called MQA.

01:22:38.750 --> 01:22:41.840
And the key idea
here, hopefully, you

01:22:41.840 --> 01:22:45.380
see from this figure
back here that, really,

01:22:45.380 --> 01:22:49.020
the part that's really bad
is the keys and the values.

01:22:49.020 --> 01:22:51.230
They have this KV cache
thing being built up,

01:22:51.230 --> 01:22:53.070
and there's memory
moving in and out.

01:22:53.070 --> 01:22:56.940
So what you do is you can have
multiple heads for the query,

01:22:56.940 --> 01:23:00.470
multiple query heads, but
only one dimension or one

01:23:00.470 --> 01:23:02.430
head for the keys and values.

01:23:02.430 --> 01:23:04.380
This immensely
simplifies things.

01:23:04.380 --> 01:23:07.580
Once you do this, now, you're
moving much less information

01:23:07.580 --> 01:23:08.820
for the K's and the V's.

01:23:08.820 --> 01:23:11.680
And so K and V is shared.

01:23:11.680 --> 01:23:13.760
But query has many heads.

01:23:13.760 --> 01:23:18.280
And so you still have multi-head
attention or multiple queries,

01:23:18.280 --> 01:23:20.120
but only single K's and V's.

01:23:20.120 --> 01:23:22.610
So that's why it's called
multi-query attention.

01:23:22.610 --> 01:23:25.540
And now, when you do the same
kind of arithmetic we have fewer

01:23:25.540 --> 01:23:28.190
memory accesses because we've
shared the K's and the V's.

01:23:28.190 --> 01:23:31.400
And the arithmetic intensity
is much, much better behaved.

01:23:31.400 --> 01:23:34.480
And so we can
increase things like--

01:23:34.480 --> 01:23:36.830
we've decreased the first
term by a factor of n.

01:23:36.830 --> 01:23:39.020
So longer sequence
lengths are now viable.

01:23:39.020 --> 01:23:41.900
And the second term is now
divided by the number of heads.

01:23:41.900 --> 01:23:43.940
So this term is also
not so terrible.

01:23:43.940 --> 01:23:46.190
So all the different
terms are controlled now.

01:23:46.190 --> 01:23:50.470
And MQA can give you
much better behaviors.

01:23:50.470 --> 01:23:55.270
GQA or group query attention
basically changes this slightly.

01:23:55.270 --> 01:23:58.570
Instead of having single query--

01:23:58.570 --> 01:24:01.580
or sorry, multiple
query and single key,

01:24:01.580 --> 01:24:04.670
you can reduce the number
of keys by some multiple.

01:24:04.670 --> 01:24:06.250
And so this will
let you trade off

01:24:06.250 --> 01:24:09.748
between the inference time
behaviors and the expressiveness

01:24:09.748 --> 01:24:10.290
of the model.

01:24:10.290 --> 01:24:12.200
Because maybe going
from multi-head

01:24:12.200 --> 01:24:17.480
all the way to multi-query is
a little bit too aggressive.

01:24:17.480 --> 01:24:22.050
Some works show that
GQA doesn't hurt,

01:24:22.050 --> 01:24:23.460
but multi-head attention hurts.

01:24:23.460 --> 01:24:25.080
I'm not going to get into that.

01:24:25.080 --> 01:24:28.070
I'm just going to close off
with this very last thing, which

01:24:28.070 --> 01:24:30.230
I think is a really
interesting development

01:24:30.230 --> 01:24:32.180
in the last few months.

01:24:32.180 --> 01:24:38.210
So back in 2019, OpenAI had this
cool paper basically arguing

01:24:38.210 --> 01:24:40.890
how to build longer
attention models.

01:24:40.890 --> 01:24:42.660
And they were basically
arguing, well,

01:24:42.660 --> 01:24:47.150
one way to do that is to come up
with sparse attention patterns.

01:24:47.150 --> 01:24:49.560
So instead of paying
attention to all the sequence,

01:24:49.560 --> 01:24:51.268
I'm going to pay
attention to, let's say,

01:24:51.268 --> 01:24:53.040
a local window at each chunk.

01:24:53.040 --> 01:24:56.900
And then I can have
other attention patterns

01:24:56.900 --> 01:24:58.610
that are like
diagonals that help

01:24:58.610 --> 01:25:00.342
propagate information across.

01:25:00.342 --> 01:25:02.300
So you can build sparse
or structured attention

01:25:02.300 --> 01:25:05.820
that trades off various kinds of
expressiveness versus runtime.

01:25:05.820 --> 01:25:08.140
GPT-3 three uses
exactly these kinds

01:25:08.140 --> 01:25:10.840
of tricks when they
originally released it to get

01:25:10.840 --> 01:25:13.480
larger attention windows.

01:25:13.480 --> 01:25:15.580
Sliding window attention
is another variant

01:25:15.580 --> 01:25:18.650
of this idea, where
at each layer,

01:25:18.650 --> 01:25:21.400
you only pay attention
to a small region

01:25:21.400 --> 01:25:23.350
around your current position.

01:25:23.350 --> 01:25:26.680
And this also is going to
control the total amount

01:25:26.680 --> 01:25:30.108
of resources that you need--

01:25:30.108 --> 01:25:31.900
total amount of resources
you need in order

01:25:31.900 --> 01:25:32.960
to do longer contacts.

01:25:32.960 --> 01:25:36.640
So your effective receptive
field is now the local 1 times

01:25:36.640 --> 01:25:39.730
the layers.

01:25:39.730 --> 01:25:43.280
The final trick-- so those
were the older ideas.

01:25:43.280 --> 01:25:46.420
But the way that this has kind
of been modern instantiation

01:25:46.420 --> 01:25:49.690
is some of the recent
papers like LLaMA 4

01:25:49.690 --> 01:25:53.470
and Gemma and Cohere
command A have now

01:25:53.470 --> 01:25:57.160
come up with this very clever
trick of basically having

01:25:57.160 --> 01:26:00.410
transformer blocks,
where in this case,

01:26:00.410 --> 01:26:03.440
you have a set of four
transformer blocks.

01:26:03.440 --> 01:26:06.270
The very bottom one
uses full self-attention

01:26:06.270 --> 01:26:07.870
with no position embedding.

01:26:07.870 --> 01:26:09.340
So there's no RoPE, no nothing.

01:26:09.340 --> 01:26:10.570
It doesn't know about
position at all.

01:26:10.570 --> 01:26:11.778
But it's full self-attention.

01:26:11.778 --> 01:26:14.110
And it only happens
once every four blocks.

01:26:14.110 --> 01:26:16.410
And then the three
blocks above it

01:26:16.410 --> 01:26:19.000
use sliding window
attention with RoPE.

01:26:19.000 --> 01:26:21.870
And so this is actually
a really clever trick

01:26:21.870 --> 01:26:24.160
to both control the
systems aspect of things

01:26:24.160 --> 01:26:27.990
because the full attention only
happens every now and then.

01:26:27.990 --> 01:26:30.520
And also, the length
extrapolation aspect

01:26:30.520 --> 01:26:33.690
because RoPE only deals
with local context windows.

01:26:33.690 --> 01:26:35.460
And anything that's
really, really long

01:26:35.460 --> 01:26:37.900
range has no position
embeddings at all

01:26:37.900 --> 01:26:40.720
so it could extrapolate
very, very aggressively.

01:26:40.720 --> 01:26:42.840
Because you don't have
to do this position

01:26:42.840 --> 01:26:45.252
extrapolation that you do
with something like RoPE.

01:26:45.252 --> 01:26:46.710
So that's a really
cool development

01:26:46.710 --> 01:26:50.040
that we've seen in the
last couple of months.

01:26:50.040 --> 01:26:52.380
So I think we're
coming up on time.

01:26:52.380 --> 01:26:54.600
Feel free to ask me
questions about architecture

01:26:54.600 --> 01:26:55.740
or hyperparameters.

01:26:55.740 --> 01:26:58.400
I'll be happy to
answer questions after.
